INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_59',
  'A security engineer needs to ensure that AWS CloudTrail logs across multiple accounts in an AWS Organization are centrally managed, immutable, and cannot be disabled by individual account administrators. Which two features of AWS CloudTrail should be configured to meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable CloudTrail data events for all services.","Configure an organization trail.","Enable CloudTrail log file validation.","Integrate CloudTrail with Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose.","Create per-account trails in each member account."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) - Configuring an organization trail ensures that CloudTrail logs are centrally managed by the management account and cannot be disabled by member accounts, providing a unified and tamper-resistant audit trail across the organization. Option C (correct) - Enabling CloudTrail log file validation ensures the integrity of the logs. It creates a hash for each log file and a signed digest, allowing verification that log files have not been modified or deleted after delivery to Amazon S3, thus ensuring immutability. Option A (incorrect) - Enabling CloudTrail data events for all services would generate extremely high volumes of logs and incur significant costs (anti-pattern ap-2.2-001), and it does not directly address central management or immutability. Option D (incorrect) - Integrating CloudTrail with Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is a valid way to stream logs for real-time processing, but it does not inherently provide central management across an organization or log file validation for immutability. Option E (incorrect) - Creating per-account trails in each member account is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-002) because it requires individual configuration and can be disabled by account administrators, failing to meet the central management and tamper-resistance requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_60',
  'To ensure that security logs are not tampered with after being delivered to Amazon S3, an organization needs to implement a mechanism for verifying log file integrity. Which AWS services or features are essential for this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudTrail log file validation","Amazon S3 Object Lock","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon EventBridge","AWS Security Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudTrail log file validation) is a critical feature that creates a hash for each log file and a signed digest file, allowing you to verify that CloudTrail logs have not been modified or deleted after delivery to your S3 bucket. This directly addresses log file integrity. Option B (Amazon S3 Object Lock) provides WORM (Write Once, Read Many) protection for objects stored in S3. By applying Object Lock, log files become immutable for a specified retention period, preventing accidental or malicious deletion or modification. This is essential for ensuring log integrity and compliance. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) stores logs but doesn''t provide the same log file validation or immutability features as CloudTrail and S3 Object Lock for S3-delivered logs. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is an event routing service and does not contribute to log file integrity. Option E (AWS Security Lake) centralizes logs but relies on underlying S3 features like Object Lock for immutability and CloudTrail''s own validation for integrity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_61',
  'A large enterprise with multiple AWS accounts in an AWS Organization needs to centralize CloudTrail logs for all accounts. The solution must be centrally managed and prevent member accounts from disabling logging. Which CloudTrail configuration options should be implemented? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable an organization trail","Configure log file validation","Create per-account trails in each member account","Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Enable data events for all S3 buckets"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Enable an organization trail) is the correct approach for centralizing CloudTrail logs across multiple accounts in an AWS Organization. Organization trails are created by the management account, apply to all member accounts, and cannot be disabled by member account administrators, ensuring centralized control and compliance. Option B (Configure log file validation) is essential for ensuring the integrity of the CloudTrail logs. It creates a hash for each log file and a signed digest, allowing verification that logs have not been tampered with or deleted. This is a best practice for security and compliance. Option C (Create per-account trails in each member account) is an anti-pattern ([ap-2.1-002]) because it requires account-by-account configuration and can be disabled by individual account administrators, defeating the purpose of centralized control. Option D (Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a good practice for real-time monitoring and alerting, but it''s not the primary mechanism for centralizing logs across an organization or preventing disabling. Option E (Enable data events for all S3 buckets) is an anti-pattern ([ap-2.2-001]) as it can generate extremely high volumes and costs, and is not directly related to centralizing management or preventing disabling of the trail itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_62',
  'To ensure comprehensive auditing and security analysis, an organization needs to capture all management events and specific data events for critical resources. Which CloudTrail configurations are necessary to achieve this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable management events for the trail","Configure data events for specific S3 buckets and Lambda functions","Enable log file validation","Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Create an organization trail"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Enable management events for the trail) is crucial as management events provide visibility into management operations that are performed on resources in your AWS account, such as creating, updating, and deleting resources. This is fundamental for comprehensive auditing. Option B (Configure data events for specific S3 buckets and Lambda functions) is necessary to capture specific data events, which provide visibility into resource operations performed on or within a resource, such as S3 object-level API activity or Lambda function invocations. It''s important to be selective (as per anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001]) and enable them only for critical resources due to potential high volume and cost. Option C (Enable log file validation) is for log integrity, not for capturing specific event types. Option D (Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is for monitoring, not for defining what events are captured. Option E (Create an organization trail) is for multi-account centralization, not for defining event types within a single account or organization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_63',
  'To meet strict audit requirements, an organization must ensure that their CloudTrail logs are immutable and cannot be altered after creation. Which AWS features or services provide this capability? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3 Object Lock","AWS CloudTrail log file validation","Amazon CloudWatch Logs retention policies","AWS Security Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3 Object Lock) provides WORM (Write Once, Read Many) protection for objects stored in S3. When CloudTrail logs are delivered to an S3 bucket with Object Lock enabled, they become immutable for a specified retention period, preventing any modification or deletion. This is crucial for audit and compliance. Option B (AWS CloudTrail log file validation) is a feature that creates a hash for each log file and a signed digest file. While it doesn''t prevent modification, it allows you to *detect* if logs have been tampered with or deleted, which is a key aspect of ensuring log integrity for audit purposes. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs retention policies) only control how long logs are kept, not their immutability against tampering. Option D (AWS Security Lake) centralizes logs and stores them in S3, but relies on S3''s underlying features like Object Lock for immutability. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service, not a storage or immutability feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_64',
  'A security engineer needs to configure CloudTrail to capture detailed information about object-level API operations on a sensitive Amazon S3 bucket. Which CloudTrail configuration steps are required? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable data events for the specific S3 bucket","Ensure management events are enabled","Configure an organization trail","Enable log file validation","Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Enable data events for the specific S3 bucket) is essential because object-level API operations (e.g., GetObject, PutObject, DeleteObject) are considered data events. These must be explicitly enabled for the relevant S3 bucket to be captured by CloudTrail. Option B (Ensure management events are enabled) is also required because a CloudTrail trail must have management events enabled to function correctly and capture any events, including data events. While the focus is on data events, management events are a prerequisite for the trail to be active. Option C (Configure an organization trail) is for multi-account centralization, not for enabling specific event types. Option D (Enable log file validation) is for log integrity, not for capturing specific event types. Option E (Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is for monitoring, not for defining what events are captured.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_65',
  'To centralize security logs from multiple AWS accounts into a single Amazon S3 bucket for long-term storage and analysis, which CloudTrail configuration best practices should be followed? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Create an organization trail in the management account","Configure S3 bucket policies to grant cross-account write access","Enable CloudTrail log file validation","Create individual trails in each member account","Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Create an organization trail in the management account) is the best practice for centralizing CloudTrail logs across multiple accounts in an AWS Organization. It ensures that logs from all member accounts are delivered to a single S3 bucket, are centrally managed, and cannot be disabled by member accounts ([ap-2.1-002]). Option C (Enable CloudTrail log file validation) is crucial for ensuring the integrity of the collected logs, allowing verification that they have not been tampered with after delivery to S3. Option B (Configure S3 bucket policies to grant cross-account write access) is implicitly handled by an organization trail; the management account sets up the S3 bucket policy automatically. Manually configuring individual cross-account write access is less secure and more complex. Option D (Create individual trails in each member account) is an anti-pattern ([ap-2.1-002]) as it leads to decentralized management and potential for logs to be disabled. Option E (Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is for real-time monitoring, not the primary mechanism for long-term S3 centralization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_66',
  'A security team needs to ensure that all API activity, including read-only events, is captured and retained for at least one year for compliance. Which CloudTrail settings and S3 features should be configured? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable management events (Read and Write)","Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy for retention","Enable data events for all resources","Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Create per-account trails"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Enable management events (Read and Write)) is necessary to capture all API activity, including read-only events (e.g., DescribeInstances, ListBuckets) and write events (e.g., RunInstances, CreateBucket). This covers the broad requirement for API activity. Option B (Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy for retention) is essential for retaining logs for at least one year in a cost-effective manner. An S3 Lifecycle policy can transition logs to lower-cost storage classes like S3 Glacier and eventually expire them after the required retention period. Option C (Enable data events for all resources) is an anti-pattern ([ap-2.2-001]) due to the high volume and cost it can incur, and it''s not necessary to capture ''all API activity'' which primarily refers to management events. Option D (Deliver logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is for real-time monitoring and alerting, not for long-term cost-effective storage. Option E (Create per-account trails) is an anti-pattern ([ap-2.1-002]) for centralized management and compliance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_67',
  'To monitor and alert on potential security incidents in real-time, a security engineer wants to integrate CloudTrail logs with a monitoring service. Which AWS services can be used to achieve this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon S3","AWS Security Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) can be configured as a destination for CloudTrail logs. Once logs are in CloudWatch Logs, you can create CloudWatch Metric Filters to identify specific patterns (e.g., unauthorized API calls) and trigger alarms. Option B (Amazon EventBridge) can receive CloudTrail events directly. You can create EventBridge rules to filter specific CloudTrail events (e.g., ''DeleteBucket'' API calls) and then route them to targets like AWS Lambda functions, Amazon SNS topics, or AWS Systems Manager Automation for automated response or alerting. Option C (Amazon S3) is for long-term storage of CloudTrail logs, not for real-time monitoring and alerting. Option D (AWS Security Lake) centralizes logs, but EventBridge and CloudWatch Logs are more direct for real-time alerting. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is for delivering streaming data, not for direct real-time monitoring and alerting on CloudTrail events.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_68',
  'Which AWS services can be used to collect DNS query logs from Amazon Route 53 Resolver and ensure their long-term retention for auditing purposes? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) can be configured as a destination for Amazon Route 53 Resolver query logs. It provides centralized storage and configurable retention policies, making it suitable for auditing. Option B (Amazon S3) is another valid destination for Amazon Route 53 Resolver query logs. S3 offers highly durable, scalable, and cost-effective storage, along with features like S3 Lifecycle policies for long-term retention and S3 Object Lock for immutability, which are crucial for auditing. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and management events, not DNS query logs. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be used to deliver logs to S3 or CloudWatch Logs, but it''s an intermediary, not the primary storage service. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus service and is not used for collecting or storing DNS query logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_69',
  'A security team needs to implement log level settings and define destinations for various application logs to ensure proper security visibility. Which AWS services are suitable for collecting and routing these application logs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a centralized service for collecting, monitoring, and storing logs from various sources, including application logs. Applications can send logs directly to CloudWatch Logs, and it allows for setting retention policies. Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be used to stream application logs from various sources (e.g., agents on EC2 instances, custom applications) and deliver them to destinations like Amazon S3, Amazon OpenSearch Service, or Amazon Redshift. This provides flexibility in routing logs to different analysis or storage platforms. Option C (Amazon S3) is a storage destination for logs, but not the service that collects or routes them from applications directly. Option D (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls, not general application logs. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus, not a log collection or routing service for application logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_70',
  'To meet compliance requirements, an organization must implement lifecycle policies for their security logs, ensuring they are retained for a specific period and then automatically deleted. Which AWS services or features facilitate this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies","Amazon CloudWatch Logs retention policies","AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS Security Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies) allow you to define rules to automatically transition objects to different storage classes (e.g., S3 Glacier for archival) or expire them after a specified period. This is ideal for managing the retention and deletion of logs stored in S3. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Logs retention policies) enable you to define how long log events are retained in CloudWatch Logs. You can set a retention period (e.g., 1 year, 5 years, or indefinitely), and logs older than the specified period are automatically deleted. Option C (AWS CloudTrail Lake) stores CloudTrail events immutably, but its primary focus is not on flexible lifecycle management like S3 or CloudWatch Logs. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service, not for managing log retention. Option E (AWS Security Lake) centralizes logs in S3, relying on S3''s lifecycle policies for retention.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_71',
  'A security team needs to ensure that all AWS API calls, including those made by users and services, are logged and available for auditing. Which AWS service is specifically designed for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon S3","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","VPC Flow Logs","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudTrail) is the primary service for recording API calls and management events made in your AWS account. It provides a history of AWS API calls for auditing, security monitoring, and operational troubleshooting. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) can be configured as a destination for CloudTrail logs. This allows for centralized storage, real-time monitoring, and analysis of API call logs, making them available for auditing. Option B (Amazon S3) is a destination for CloudTrail logs, but CloudTrail itself is the service that records the API calls. Option D (VPC Flow Logs) captures network traffic information, not API calls. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus service, not for recording API calls.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_72',
  'Which AWS services can be used to centralize and store DNS query logs from Amazon Route 53 Resolver for security analysis? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) can be configured as a destination for Amazon Route 53 Resolver query logs, providing a centralized repository for these logs. This allows for analysis using CloudWatch Logs Insights. Option B (Amazon S3) is another valid destination for Amazon Route 53 Resolver query logs. Storing logs in S3 provides durable, scalable, and cost-effective long-term storage, suitable for security analysis. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls, not DNS query logs. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can deliver logs to S3 or CloudWatch Logs, but it''s an intermediary, not the primary storage service. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus service and is not used for collecting or storing DNS query logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_73',
  'Which AWS services can be used to centralize and store VPC Flow Logs for security analysis and long-term retention? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3) is a highly durable, scalable, and cost-effective object storage service that is a common and recommended destination for storing VPC Flow Logs for long-term retention and analysis. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is another valid destination for VPC Flow Logs. It provides centralized log storage and allows for real-time monitoring and analysis using CloudWatch Logs Insights. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and management events, not network flow data. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be used to stream VPC Flow Logs to S3 or CloudWatch Logs, but it''s an intermediary service, not the primary storage for the logs themselves. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus service and is not directly involved in storing VPC Flow Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_74',
  'A company requires a solution to centralize audit logs from multiple AWS accounts within an AWS Organization. The solution must ensure that logs are immutable, tamper-evident, and cannot be disabled by individual account administrators. Which two features are crucial for meeting these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CloudTrail organization trail","CloudTrail log file validation","Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage","Amazon EventBridge rules for log delivery","CloudWatch Logs metric filters"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (CloudTrail organization trail) is crucial because it allows a management account to create a trail that logs events for all accounts in the organization, and member accounts cannot disable it. This centralizes logs and prevents individual account administrators from tampering with the logging configuration (anti-pattern: ap-2.1-002). Option B (CloudTrail log file validation) ensures that log files have not been tampered with or deleted after they are delivered to the S3 bucket, providing tamper-evidence and immutability (anti-pattern: ap-2.1-001). Option C (Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage) is a cost-effective storage class for long-term retention but does not inherently provide the centralized, tamper-evident logging mechanism itself. Option D (Amazon EventBridge rules for log delivery) can route events but is not the primary mechanism for ensuring centralized, immutable CloudTrail logs across an organization. Option E (CloudWatch Logs metric filters) are for creating alarms based on log patterns, not for ensuring the integrity or centralization of the log files themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_75',
  'A security team is implementing a logging strategy for a new application deployed on Amazon EC2 instances within a VPC. They need to capture network traffic information, including source/destination IP addresses, ports, and protocols, for security analysis and troubleshooting. Which two services should be enabled to collect this network flow data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Flow Logs","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (VPC Flow Logs) captures detailed information about IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in a VPC, including source/destination IP, ports, and protocols. This is precisely the network flow data required. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a common destination for VPC Flow Logs, allowing for centralized storage, monitoring, and analysis of the flow log data. Option B (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events made in your AWS account, not network traffic flow. Option D (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service that analyzes various data sources, including VPC Flow Logs, but it is not the service that *collects* the flow logs. Option E (AWS Config) records configuration changes to AWS resources, not network traffic flow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_76',
  'A security team is designing a logging solution for a multi-account AWS environment. They want to ensure that all API activity across all accounts in their AWS Organization is centrally logged and cannot be disabled by individual member accounts. Which two CloudTrail features are essential for this design? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Organization trail","S3 bucket policy for central log delivery","CloudTrail log file validation","CloudWatch Logs integration","Per-account trails"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Organization trail) is essential because it allows a management account to create a single trail that logs events for all accounts in the organization, and member accounts cannot disable it. This centralizes logs and prevents individual account administrators from tampering with the logging configuration (anti-pattern: ap-2.1-002). Option B (S3 bucket policy for central log delivery) is crucial. The central S3 bucket designated for the organization trail must have the correct bucket policy to allow CloudTrail from all member accounts to write logs, ensuring centralized delivery. Option C (CloudTrail log file validation) ensures log integrity but is not directly about centralizing logs across accounts. Option D (CloudWatch Logs integration) is for monitoring and alerting on logs, not for centralizing the raw log files. Option E (Per-account trails) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-002) as it requires individual configuration and can be disabled by member accounts, failing the centralization and tamper-resistance requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_77',
  'A company needs to implement a comprehensive logging strategy that includes capturing DNS query logs for security analysis and anomaly detection. They are using Amazon Route 53 for DNS resolution. Which two services are required to collect and store these DNS query logs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Route 53 Resolver query logging","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon VPC Flow Logs","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Route 53 Resolver query logging) is the specific feature in Amazon Route 53 that enables logging of DNS queries made to Route 53 Resolvers. This captures the detailed DNS query information. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is the primary destination for Route 53 Resolver query logs. Once in CloudWatch Logs, these logs can be monitored, analyzed, and retained. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) logs API calls made to Route 53, not the DNS queries themselves. Option D (Amazon VPC Flow Logs) captures network traffic flow, not DNS queries. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be used to stream logs to other destinations, but CloudWatch Logs is the direct and often default destination for Route 53 query logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_78',
  'A company needs to ensure that their CloudTrail logs are delivered to a central Amazon S3 bucket for long-term archival and analysis. They also want to stream these logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs for real-time monitoring and alerting. Which two configurations are required for this setup? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure the CloudTrail trail to deliver logs to an S3 bucket","Configure the CloudTrail trail to send events to CloudWatch Logs","Enable S3 server access logging for the CloudTrail S3 bucket","Set up an Amazon EventBridge rule to forward S3 events to CloudWatch Logs","Enable CloudTrail log file validation"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Configure the CloudTrail trail to deliver logs to an S3 bucket) is the primary method for long-term archival of CloudTrail logs. This ensures the raw log files are stored durably. Option B (Configure the CloudTrail trail to send events to CloudWatch Logs) is a direct option within CloudTrail configuration to stream events to CloudWatch Logs for real-time monitoring, metric filtering, and alarming. Option C (Enable S3 server access logging for the CloudTrail S3 bucket) would log access to the CloudTrail S3 bucket itself, not the CloudTrail events. Option D (Set up an Amazon EventBridge rule to forward S3 events to CloudWatch Logs) is an indirect and less efficient method; CloudTrail has a direct integration with CloudWatch Logs. Option E (Enable CloudTrail log file validation) ensures log integrity but does not define the log delivery destinations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_79',
  'A security team needs to centralize AWS CloudTrail logs from multiple AWS accounts within an AWS Organization for auditing and compliance. They want to ensure that member accounts cannot disable logging. Which two actions should they take to meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Create a multi-region trail in each member account and configure Amazon S3 bucket policies to prevent deletion.","Enable AWS CloudTrail log file validation for each individual trail.","Create an organization trail from the management account, configured to deliver logs to a central Amazon S3 bucket.","Configure Amazon EventBridge rules in each account to forward AWS CloudTrail events to a central Amazon SQS queue.","Ensure the central Amazon S3 bucket has a bucket policy that denies `s3:DeleteObject` actions for all users except a designated auditing role."]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective way to centralize CloudTrail logs from multiple accounts with minimal overhead and prevent member accounts from disabling logging. An organization trail, created from the management account, automatically applies to all member accounts and cannot be disabled by them. Option E (correct) ensures the immutability of the logs once delivered to the central Amazon S3 bucket by preventing unauthorized deletion, which is crucial for auditing and compliance. Option A (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-002) as creating per-account trails is operationally intensive and member accounts can disable them. Option B (incorrect) is a good security practice but does not address the centralization or prevention of disabling logs by member accounts. Option D (incorrect) would add significant operational overhead for forwarding logs and does not inherently prevent disabling of the original CloudTrail trails.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_80',
  'Which two statements accurately describe the capabilities of AWS CloudTrail log file validation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It verifies the integrity of log files after they have been delivered to Amazon S3.","It prevents unauthorized users from deleting AWS CloudTrail log files from Amazon S3.","It generates a hash for each log file and a signed digest file for verification.","It automatically encrypts AWS CloudTrail logs at rest using AWS KMS.","It ensures that AWS CloudTrail logs are delivered to Amazon CloudWatch Logs in real-time."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes log file validation''s primary purpose: to verify that log files have not been tampered with or deleted after delivery to Amazon S3. Option C (correct) explains the mechanism: validation creates a hash for each log file and a signed digest, allowing verification that log files have not been modified or deleted. Option B (incorrect) is not the function of log file validation; S3 bucket policies or S3 Object Lock are used for preventing deletion. Option D (incorrect) describes encryption, which is a separate feature from log file validation. Option E (incorrect) describes CloudWatch Logs integration, not log file validation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_81',
  'Which two Amazon S3 features are essential for ensuring the immutability and long-term retention of audit logs, such as those from AWS CloudTrail? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable S3 Object Lock in WORM (Write Once Read Many) mode.","Configure S3 Lifecycle policies to transition logs to Amazon S3 Glacier after 90 days.","Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket.","Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication to a different AWS Region.","Apply a bucket policy that explicitly denies `s3:DeleteObject` actions for all users except a designated auditing role."]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (correct) enables S3 Object Lock in WORM mode, which prevents objects from being overwritten or deleted for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely, ensuring immutability for compliance. Option E (correct) applies a bucket policy to explicitly deny `s3:DeleteObject` actions, which is a fundamental control to prevent accidental or malicious deletion of logs. Option B (incorrect) is for cost optimization and retention management, but does not inherently ensure immutability against deletion. Option C (incorrect) protects against accidental overwrites by keeping previous versions, but does not prevent deletion of all versions if permissions allow. Option D (incorrect) provides disaster recovery and redundancy but doesn''t, by itself, enforce immutability or prevent deletion from both regions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_82',
  'Which two statements are true regarding the integration of AWS CloudTrail with Amazon CloudWatch Logs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudTrail automatically sends all management events to Amazon CloudWatch Logs by default.","Integrating AWS CloudTrail with Amazon CloudWatch Logs allows for real-time monitoring and alerting on API activity.","AWS CloudTrail data events cannot be sent to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.","Amazon CloudWatch Logs can be used to create metric filters and alarms based on AWS CloudTrail events.","AWS CloudTrail logs are stored in Amazon CloudWatch Logs for long-term archival by default."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) highlights a key benefit: integrating CloudTrail with CloudWatch Logs enables real-time monitoring of API activity, which is crucial for security. Option D (correct) specifies that CloudWatch Logs can be used to create metric filters and alarms based on CloudTrail events, allowing for automated alerting on specific security-related API calls. Option A (incorrect) is false; CloudTrail does not automatically send logs to CloudWatch Logs by default; it must be explicitly configured. Option C (incorrect) is false; CloudTrail data events can indeed be sent to CloudWatch Logs if configured. Option E (incorrect) is false; CloudTrail logs are primarily archived in Amazon S3, not CloudWatch Logs, for long-term retention.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_83',
  'Which two AWS services or features are primarily used to capture and store network traffic metadata (like source/destination IP, port, and protocol) for security analysis and auditing within a VPC? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable Amazon VPC Flow Logs for all VPCs and send them to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.","Enable AWS CloudTrail management events for all API calls related to network configurations.","Enable Amazon VPC Flow Logs for all VPCs and send them to an Amazon S3 bucket.","Configure AWS Config rules to monitor changes to network ACLs and security groups.","Enable Amazon Route 53 DNS query logging for all hosted zones."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) describes enabling Amazon VPC Flow Logs and sending them to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, which allows for real-time monitoring, analysis, and alerting on network traffic metadata. Option C (correct) also describes enabling Amazon VPC Flow Logs but sending them to an Amazon S3 bucket, which is ideal for long-term archival and detailed historical analysis using services like Amazon Athena. Both options utilize VPC Flow Logs, which are the core service for capturing network traffic metadata. Option B (incorrect) captures API calls related to network configurations, not the actual network traffic metadata itself. Option D (incorrect) monitors configuration changes, not traffic. Option E (incorrect) captures DNS queries, which is different from IP traffic metadata.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_84',
  'A security administrator is implementing a logging solution for a multi-account AWS environment. Which characteristics are true for an AWS CloudTrail organization trail compared to per-account trails? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It aggregates logs from all member accounts into a central Amazon S3 bucket.","It can be disabled by individual member account administrators.","It is centrally managed by the management account.","It requires separate configuration for each member account.","It only captures management events, not data events."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) states that an organization trail aggregates logs from all member accounts into a central Amazon S3 bucket, which is a key benefit for centralized security monitoring. Option C (correct) correctly identifies that an organization trail is centrally managed by the management account, ensuring consistent logging across the organization. Option B (incorrect) is a common misconception (anti-pattern ap-2.1-002); organization trails cannot be disabled by individual member account administrators, enhancing security. Option D (incorrect) is false; organization trails simplify configuration by applying to all accounts automatically. Option E (incorrect) is false; organization trails can capture both management and data events, just like single-account trails, depending on configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_85',
  'To ensure the integrity and immutability of AWS CloudTrail logs for auditing and compliance, which features and configurations should be implemented? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable CloudTrail log file validation.","Store logs in an Amazon S3 bucket with versioning enabled.","Configure an Amazon S3 bucket policy to prevent deletion or modification.","Use AWS Config to monitor CloudTrail trail settings.","Encrypt logs using AWS KMS managed keys."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) states to enable CloudTrail log file validation. Without log file validation (anti-pattern ap-2.1-001), tampered or deleted CloudTrail logs are undetectable. Validation creates a hash for each log file and a signed digest, allowing verification that log files have not been modified or deleted. Option C (correct) states to configure an Amazon S3 bucket policy to prevent deletion or modification, which is crucial for log immutability and compliance. This policy can enforce write-once-read-many (WORM) behavior. Option B (incorrect) states to store logs in an Amazon S3 bucket with versioning enabled. While S3 versioning helps recover from accidental deletions, it does not prevent modification or malicious deletion of specific versions, nor does it provide cryptographic proof of integrity like log file validation. Option D (incorrect) states to use AWS Config to monitor CloudTrail trail settings. AWS Config can monitor the configuration of the trail itself, but it does not directly ensure the integrity of the log files once delivered to S3. Option E (incorrect) states to encrypt logs using AWS KMS managed keys. Encryption protects data confidentiality but does not inherently guarantee log file integrity against tampering or deletion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_86',
  'When configuring AWS CloudTrail, which types of events can be captured to provide detailed visibility into resource changes and data plane operations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Management events","Data events","System events","Application events","Network events"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) refers to Management events, which provide visibility into management operations performed on resources in your AWS account, such as creating an Amazon S3 bucket or launching an Amazon EC2 instance. Option B (correct) refers to Data events, which provide visibility into resource operations performed on or within a resource, such as Amazon S3 object-level API activity (e.g., GetObject, PutObject) or AWS Lambda function invocations. Option C (incorrect) ''System events'' is not a standard CloudTrail event type. Option D (incorrect) ''Application events'' is too generic and not a specific CloudTrail category. Option E (incorrect) ''Network events'' is also too generic; network-related API calls would fall under management events, and VPC Flow Logs capture network traffic details, which are separate from CloudTrail.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_87',
  'A company needs to centralize VPC Flow Logs from multiple VPCs for security analysis and long-term retention. Which AWS services can be used as destinations for VPC Flow Logs to meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states Amazon CloudWatch Logs, which is a supported destination for VPC Flow Logs, allowing for real-time monitoring, metric creation, and log insights. Option B (correct) states Amazon S3, which is also a supported destination for VPC Flow Logs, ideal for long-term archival and analysis using services like Amazon Athena. Option C (incorrect) states AWS CloudTrail. CloudTrail records API calls and management events, not network flow data. Option D (incorrect) states Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. While Kinesis Data Firehose can deliver logs, Kinesis Data Streams is not a direct destination for VPC Flow Logs. Option E (incorrect) states AWS Config, which assesses and audits resource configurations, not a destination for network flow logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_3_88',
  'A security team wants to ensure that all AWS accounts in their organization consistently enable CloudTrail logging and that these logs cannot be easily disabled by individual account administrators. Which two approaches should they implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Create an AWS CloudTrail organization trail from the management account.","Implement an AWS Organizations Service Control Policy (SCP) to deny CloudTrail disable actions.","Configure per-account CloudTrail trails in each member account.","Use AWS Config rules to detect disabled CloudTrail trails.","Enable CloudTrail log file validation in each account."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states to create an AWS CloudTrail organization trail from the management account. Organization trails are centrally managed and cannot be disabled by member accounts, ensuring consistent logging across the organization (addressing anti-pattern ap-2.1-002). Option B (correct) states to implement an AWS Organizations Service Control Policy (SCP) to deny CloudTrail disable actions. An SCP provides guardrails at the organizational level, preventing member accounts from performing actions like `cloudtrail:DeleteTrail` or `cloudtrail:StopLogging`, thus enforcing logging. Option C (incorrect) states to configure per-account CloudTrail trails in each member account. This approach is less scalable and allows individual account administrators to disable their trails, which is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-002). Option D (incorrect) states to use AWS Config rules to detect disabled CloudTrail trails. While AWS Config can detect non-compliance, it is a reactive measure and does not prevent the disabling of trails. Option E (incorrect) states to enable CloudTrail log file validation in each account. Log file validation ensures integrity but does not prevent the trail from being disabled.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_3',
  'medium'
);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_55_5
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_63',
  'A large financial institution needs to proactively identify unusual API activity that could indicate a security breach or insider threat. They have already centralized CloudTrail logs to an Amazon S3 bucket and integrated with Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The security team requires a solution that automatically detects deviations from normal API call patterns, such as an unusual number of API calls, calls from unusual locations, or calls to rarely used services, without requiring manual baseline definition. The solution must integrate with existing monitoring tools for alerting. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure Amazon CloudWatch metric filters on CloudTrail logs to detect specific API calls and create alarms.","Enable AWS CloudTrail Insights on the organization trail to automatically detect unusual API activity.","Stream CloudTrail logs to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, then to Amazon OpenSearch Service, and use OpenSearch anomaly detection.","Implement custom AWS Lambda functions to analyze CloudTrail logs in Amazon S3 for deviations from a manually defined baseline.","Integrate CloudTrail with AWS Security Lake, and then use Amazon Athena to query for known malicious patterns."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) is a direct capability of AWS CloudTrail Insights. CloudTrail Insights continuously analyzes CloudTrail management events and automatically detects unusual activity, such as spikes in API calls, errors, or unusual resource provisioning, without requiring manual baseline configuration. It integrates with Amazon EventBridge for automated alerting. Option C (correct) is a valid, albeit more complex, approach for advanced anomaly detection. Streaming logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service allows for powerful search, visualization, and built-in anomaly detection features that can identify deviations from normal patterns. This provides a highly customizable solution for proactive threat hunting. Option A (incorrect) is suitable for detecting *known* specific API calls or patterns but requires manual definition of metric filters and thresholds. It does not automatically detect *unusual* activity without pre-defined rules or baselines, which is a key requirement. Option D (incorrect) involves significant operational overhead for developing, maintaining, and scaling custom AWS Lambda functions to define and monitor baselines. CloudTrail Insights provides this capability natively and automatically. Option E (incorrect) is excellent for centralized security data aggregation and querying for *known* threats or patterns using Amazon Athena. However, it does not inherently provide automated anomaly detection for *unusual* API activity without additional custom analysis or integration with other services like Amazon OpenSearch Service or CloudTrail Insights.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_64',
  'A security engineer is designing a centralized log analysis solution for multiple AWS accounts within an AWS Organization. The solution must allow security analysts to run SQL queries against security logs to identify potential threats and anomalies. Which AWS services can facilitate this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Athena","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS Security Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon OpenSearch Service"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Amazon Athena) is a serverless query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. When integrated with AWS Security Lake, which centralizes security data, Athena can be used to query the aggregated logs. Option C (AWS Security Lake) automatically centralizes security data from AWS environments, SaaS providers, on-premises sources, and cloud sources into a purpose-built data lake. It normalizes this data to the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) format, making it readily queryable by services like Amazon Athena. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is used for interactive analysis of logs in CloudWatch Logs, but Security Lake aggregates logs from various sources beyond just CloudWatch Logs, and Athena is more suitable for direct SQL querying of a data lake. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data streaming service used for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations like Amazon S3, not for querying the data. Option E (Amazon OpenSearch Service) can be used for log analysis and dashboards, but the question specifically asks for running SQL queries against centralized security logs, which Athena excels at when paired with Security Lake.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_65',
  'Which AWS services can be used to parse and normalize security logs for analysis in a centralized security data lake? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon OpenSearch Service"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) automatically centralizes security data from various sources and normalizes it to the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) format, which inherently involves parsing and normalizing. Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be configured with AWS Lambda transformations to parse, transform, and normalize streaming data before delivering it to destinations like Amazon S3, which can then be part of a data lake. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is primarily for querying and analyzing logs already in CloudWatch Logs, not for parsing and normalizing data before ingestion into a data lake. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is a serverless event bus that routes events between AWS services, SaaS applications, and your own applications, but it doesn''t directly parse or normalize log data for a data lake. Option E (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is used for search, analysis, and visualization of data, typically after it has been parsed and normalized.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_66',
  'To enable proactive security investigation and response, a security operations center (SOC) needs to correlate security events from various sources. Which AWS services are suitable for aggregating and querying diverse security data for correlation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon Athena","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Lambda","Amazon SNS"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) is specifically designed to centralize security data from AWS, SaaS providers, and on-premises sources into a data lake, normalizing it to OCSF. This aggregation is crucial for correlating events from diverse sources. Option B (Amazon Athena) is a serverless query service that can directly query data stored in Amazon S3, including the OCSF-formatted data in AWS Security Lake. This allows security analysts to run complex SQL queries to correlate events and identify patterns across the aggregated data. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch) is primarily for monitoring and observability of AWS resources and applications, not for centralizing and querying diverse security logs from multiple sources for correlation. Option D (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service that can be used for processing data, but it''s not a primary service for data aggregation and querying for correlation. Option E (Amazon SNS) is a messaging service used for sending notifications, not for log aggregation or querying.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_67',
  'A security team wants to build custom dashboards to visualize security events and trends from their centralized logs. Which AWS services can be leveraged to create these dashboards? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon Athena","AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon OpenSearch Service) provides OpenSearch Dashboards (formerly Kibana), which is a powerful visualization tool for creating custom dashboards from data stored in OpenSearch clusters. This is ideal for visualizing security events and trends. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) allows users to query log data and create visualizations directly within the CloudWatch console based on those queries. While not as feature-rich as OpenSearch Dashboards, it can be used for basic dashboarding of CloudWatch Logs data. Option C (Amazon Athena) is a query service and does not natively provide dashboarding capabilities; it typically integrates with services like Amazon QuickSight for visualization. Option D (AWS CloudTrail Lake) provides query capabilities for CloudTrail events but does not offer native dashboarding. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is an event routing service and has no dashboarding capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_68',
  'Which AWS services can be used to identify known threats and anomalies within security logs by applying advanced analytics or querying capabilities? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon Athena","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS Lambda","Amazon SNS"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon OpenSearch Service) offers advanced analytical capabilities, including anomaly detection plugins and machine learning features, to identify known threats and unusual patterns in log data. Its powerful search and aggregation features are also key. Option B (Amazon Athena) allows security analysts to run complex SQL queries against large volumes of security logs stored in Amazon S3. By crafting specific queries, analysts can identify known threat indicators, search for specific patterns, or detect deviations from normal behavior. Option C (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service and does not provide analytical capabilities for threat identification. Option D (AWS Lambda) is a compute service that can be used to process data, but it''s not an analytical service itself for identifying threats. Option E (Amazon SNS) is a messaging service for notifications, not for threat analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_69',
  'A security team needs to automate threat hunting by regularly querying security logs for specific indicators of compromise (IOCs). Which AWS services can be used to execute these automated queries against aggregated security data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Athena","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS Lambda","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (Amazon Athena) allows users to run standard SQL queries against data in Amazon S3, which can include aggregated security logs. These queries can be automated. Option D (AWS Lambda) can be used to invoke Athena queries on a schedule (e.g., via EventBridge) or in response to events. A Lambda function can be programmed to run specific SQL queries against security logs in Athena, process the results, and trigger further actions if IOCs are found, thus automating threat hunting. Option B (Amazon EventBridge) can schedule Lambda functions, but it doesn''t execute queries itself. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is for interactive querying of CloudWatch Logs, not typically for automated, scheduled queries against a broader data lake. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is for data delivery, not query execution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_70',
  'To centralize security data aggregation in a standardized format for easier analysis, a security architect wants to use a service that automatically collects data from various AWS services and third-party sources. Which AWS services are best suited for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) is explicitly designed to centralize security data from AWS services, SaaS providers, and on-premises sources, and normalize it to the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) format. This directly addresses the requirement for standardized aggregation. Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be used to stream data from various sources (including custom applications or other AWS services) and deliver it to destinations like Amazon S3. While it requires configuration for normalization (e.g., via Lambda transformations), it is a key component for aggregating diverse data streams into a centralized storage for analysis. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) aggregates logs from many AWS services but doesn''t normalize them to OCSF or easily ingest from third-party sources directly into a data lake. Option D (Amazon S3) is a storage service, not an aggregation or normalization service itself. Option E (AWS CloudTrail Lake) centralizes CloudTrail events specifically, not a broad range of security data from diverse sources including third-parties and SaaS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_71',
  'To enhance proactive security, a security analyst needs to perform ad-hoc queries on security logs stored in Amazon S3, which are part of a data lake. Which AWS services enable interactive SQL querying of this data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Athena","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Athena) is a serverless query service that allows running standard SQL queries directly on data stored in Amazon S3 without provisioning any infrastructure. This is ideal for ad-hoc analysis of security logs in a data lake. Option B (Amazon OpenSearch Service) with its SQL plugin allows users to query data in OpenSearch clusters using SQL. If the S3 data is ingested into OpenSearch, this provides another powerful interactive SQL querying option. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is specifically for querying logs within Amazon CloudWatch Logs, not directly for data in Amazon S3. Option D (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is for querying CloudTrail events, not general security logs stored in S3. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service, not a query service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_72',
  'A security team wants to centralize all security-related logs from various AWS accounts and services into a single data lake for comprehensive analysis. Which AWS services are crucial for building this centralized security data lake? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon S3","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon OpenSearch Service"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) is designed to automatically centralize security data from AWS environments, SaaS providers, and on-premises sources into a purpose-built data lake, normalizing it to OCSF. This service is foundational for a centralized security data lake. Option B (Amazon S3) is the underlying object storage service where AWS Security Lake stores the aggregated and normalized security data. It provides the scalable, durable, and cost-effective storage for the data lake itself. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) aggregates logs from various AWS services but is not the primary component for building a centralized *data lake* that includes third-party sources and OCSF normalization. Option D (AWS CloudTrail Lake) focuses specifically on CloudTrail events, not a broad range of security logs from diverse sources. Option E (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is an analytics and visualization service, typically used *after* data is aggregated into a data lake, not for building the data lake itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_73',
  'Which AWS services can be integrated with AWS Security Lake to enable advanced security analytics and threat intelligence capabilities? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon Athena","AWS Lambda","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SNS"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon OpenSearch Service) can ingest data from AWS Security Lake (via S3) and provide advanced search, analysis, and visualization capabilities, including anomaly detection and threat intelligence integrations. Option B (Amazon Athena) can directly query the OCSF-formatted data stored by AWS Security Lake in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. This allows for ad-hoc analysis, threat hunting, and integration with other tools for threat intelligence. Option C (AWS Lambda) can be used to process data or trigger actions based on findings, but it''s not an analytics service itself for threat intelligence. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus for routing events, not for advanced analytics. Option E (Amazon SNS) is a messaging service for notifications, not for analytics or threat intelligence.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_74',
  'Which AWS services are essential for building a robust security information and event management (SIEM) solution on AWS, enabling log aggregation and analysis? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS Security Lake","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon EventBridge","AWS Lambda"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is a common component of SIEM solutions on AWS, providing powerful search, analysis, and visualization capabilities for large volumes of log data. It''s often used for real-time monitoring and threat detection. Option B (AWS Security Lake) centralizes security data from various sources into a data lake, normalizing it to OCSF. This provides the foundational aggregated and standardized data source that a SIEM solution, such as one built on OpenSearch Service, can then consume and analyze. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) aggregates logs but is not a full SIEM solution itself, nor does it normalize data to OCSF or integrate as broadly as Security Lake. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is for event routing and automation, not core SIEM functionality. Option E (AWS Lambda) is a compute service that can be part of a SIEM pipeline for data processing, but not a core SIEM service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_75',
  'A security team needs to perform forensic investigations by querying historical security logs that are stored in a centralized data lake. Which AWS services enable efficient querying of this large volume of historical data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Athena","AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Athena) is a serverless query service that allows running standard SQL queries directly on large volumes of data stored in Amazon S3, which is typically where historical logs in a data lake reside. This makes it highly efficient for forensic investigations. Option B (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is specifically designed for immutable storage and querying of CloudTrail events, which are critical for forensic investigations into API activity. It allows for efficient querying of historical CloudTrail data. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is for querying logs within CloudWatch Logs, which might not hold all historical data from a centralized data lake. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service, not a query service. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is an event routing service, not for querying historical data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_76',
  'To ensure compliance and facilitate security audits, an organization needs to retain security logs for several years in a cost-effective manner. Which AWS services are suitable for long-term, immutable storage of these logs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS Security Lake","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon OpenSearch Service"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3) is an ideal service for long-term, cost-effective, and highly durable storage of logs. It supports S3 Glacier storage classes for archival and S3 Object Lock for immutability, which is crucial for compliance. Option B (AWS Security Lake) stores its aggregated and normalized security data in Amazon S3. By using Security Lake, logs are automatically centralized and stored in S3, benefiting from S3''s long-term retention and immutability features. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) can retain logs for a specified period, but for very long-term, cost-optimized archival with strong immutability guarantees (like S3 Object Lock), S3 is generally preferred. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service, not a storage service. Option E (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is for active analysis and visualization, not for long-term, cost-effective archival storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_77',
  'A security team wants to build custom security dashboards that aggregate data from various log sources, including CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and GuardDuty findings. Which AWS services can be used to collect and visualize this diverse log data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SNS"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) is designed to centralize security data from various AWS services (like CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, GuardDuty) and normalize it to OCSF, making it ready for analysis. This provides the aggregated data source. Option B (Amazon OpenSearch Service) provides powerful search, analysis, and visualization capabilities through OpenSearch Dashboards (Kibana). It can ingest data from S3 (where Security Lake stores its data) and create custom dashboards to visualize the aggregated security events and findings. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is for querying CloudWatch Logs, but it''s not a comprehensive dashboarding solution for diverse data sources aggregated in a data lake. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is for routing events, not for data collection and visualization. Option E (Amazon SNS) is a messaging service, not for log collection or visualization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_78',
  'Which AWS services are suitable for parsing and enriching security logs with additional context (e.g., geolocation, threat intelligence) before they are stored in a centralized repository? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Lambda","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon Athena","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS Security Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Lambda) functions can be used to process log data as it flows through a pipeline. A Lambda function can be invoked to parse raw logs, query external threat intelligence feeds or geolocation databases, and then enrich the log records before they are stored. Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be configured to invoke a Lambda function for data transformation. This allows Firehose to deliver data to a destination after it has been parsed and enriched by the Lambda function. Option C (Amazon Athena) is a query service and does not perform data parsing or enrichment during ingestion. Option D (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is for analyzing logs already stored in CloudWatch Logs, not for pre-ingestion enrichment. Option E (AWS Security Lake) performs automatic normalization to OCSF, but for custom enrichment beyond OCSF, Lambda and Kinesis Data Firehose offer the flexibility.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_79',
  'To ensure comprehensive security visibility, a company needs to centralize DNS query logs from Amazon Route 53 Resolver into a security data lake. Which AWS services can be used to achieve this centralization and make the logs queryable? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon Athena","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon S3","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) can ingest DNS query logs from Amazon Route 53 Resolver, normalize them to OCSF, and store them in a centralized S3 bucket, making them part of the security data lake. Option B (Amazon Athena) can then be used to query these DNS logs stored in Amazon S3, whether directly or through AWS Security Lake, using standard SQL for security analysis and threat hunting. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) can receive Route 53 Resolver query logs, but Security Lake provides broader centralization and OCSF normalization for a data lake. Option D (Amazon S3) is the storage layer for the data lake, but not the service that centralizes or makes them queryable directly. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can deliver logs to S3, but Security Lake provides the automated centralization and normalization aspect.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_80',
  'A security engineer needs to analyze security events from various AWS services and third-party applications in a unified manner. Which AWS services help in aggregating these diverse logs and providing a unified query interface? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon EventBridge","AWS Lambda"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) is designed to centralize security data from AWS services and third-party applications, normalizing it to the OCSF format. This provides a unified data source. Option B (Amazon OpenSearch Service) can ingest this aggregated and normalized data (from S3 where Security Lake stores it) and provide a powerful, unified query interface (OpenSearch Dashboards) for analysis across all log sources. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) queries only CloudWatch Logs, not diverse third-party sources. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is for event routing, not aggregation and querying. Option E (AWS Lambda) is a compute service, not a unified query interface for logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_81',
  'A security analyst needs to quickly identify suspicious activities within CloudTrail logs, such as unauthorized API calls or changes to security configurations. They require a solution that allows for real-time alerting and automated response. Which two services are essential for setting up such a monitoring and alerting system? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is essential for ingesting and monitoring CloudTrail logs. Once logs are in CloudWatch Logs, metric filters can be created to identify specific patterns or events, triggering alarms. Option B (Amazon EventBridge) can be used to create rules that react to specific CloudWatch Alarms or direct CloudTrail events, enabling automated responses such as invoking AWS Lambda functions or sending notifications. Option C (Amazon S3) is typically used for long-term storage of CloudTrail logs but does not provide real-time alerting or automated response capabilities directly. Option D (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is for immutable storage and analysis of CloudTrail events, but for real-time alerting and automated response based on specific event patterns, CloudWatch Logs and EventBridge are more direct. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is for streaming data to destinations, not for direct real-time alerting and automated response based on log patterns.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_82',
  'A security team needs to analyze application logs stored in Amazon CloudWatch Logs to identify potential security threats and anomalies. They require a solution that allows them to perform complex queries and visualize the data without exporting it to another service. Which two services are best suited for this task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards","Amazon Athena","Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS CloudTrail Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is specifically designed for interactive querying and analysis of log data in CloudWatch Logs. It allows users to perform complex queries, discover fields, and visualize log data directly within the CloudWatch console. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards) can be used to visualize the results of CloudWatch Logs Insights queries, as well as other CloudWatch metrics and alarms, providing a centralized view for monitoring and identifying trends. Option C (Amazon Athena) is for querying data in Amazon S3, not directly for logs stored in CloudWatch Logs. Option D (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is a powerful analytics engine but requires provisioning and managing clusters, and logs would need to be streamed to it, adding complexity. Option E (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is for analyzing CloudTrail events, not general application logs stored in CloudWatch Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_83',
  'A company is migrating its on-premises applications to AWS and needs to ensure that all security-related logs, including those from custom applications, are centralized and searchable. They want a solution that supports real-time ingestion and advanced analytics capabilities. Which two services can facilitate this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is ideal for centralizing logs from various sources, including custom applications, EC2 instances, and other AWS services. It provides real-time monitoring and can store logs for long periods. Option B (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is a powerful, fully managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch clusters. It is excellent for advanced analytics, full-text search, and visualization of log data, often used as a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) solution. Logs from CloudWatch Logs can be streamed to OpenSearch Service. Option C (Amazon S3) is a durable object storage service, suitable for long-term archiving of logs, but it does not provide real-time ingestion or advanced analytics capabilities directly. Option D (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and events in your AWS account, not custom application logs. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data streaming service that can deliver logs to destinations like S3 or OpenSearch Service, but it is not the primary service for log centralization and analytics itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_84',
  'A security engineer needs to set up automated threat hunting based on security events. They want to correlate logs from various sources, identify known threats, and visualize the findings. Which two services are key components for building such an automated threat hunting platform? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS Security Lake","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon EventBridge","AWS CloudTrail Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is a powerful platform for log analytics, full-text search, and visualization, making it excellent for correlating diverse log sources, identifying patterns, and visualizing threat data. Option B (AWS Security Lake) centralizes security data from various sources (CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, GuardDuty, etc.) into an S3 data lake in OCSF format, providing a normalized and aggregated dataset that is ideal for threat hunting and feeding into analytics platforms like OpenSearch Service. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is great for querying CloudWatch Logs, but for broader correlation across diverse data sources and advanced threat hunting, OpenSearch Service and Security Lake offer more comprehensive capabilities. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is for event routing and automated responses, not for threat hunting analysis itself. Option E (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is for analyzing CloudTrail events, which is a subset of the data needed for comprehensive threat hunting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_85',
  'A company needs to implement proactive security investigation and response capabilities. This involves aggregating security data, identifying anomalies, and triggering automated actions based on specific insights. Which two services are fundamental for enabling proactive security investigation and response workflows? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Hub","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Hub) provides a centralized view of security findings from various AWS services and allows for custom insights, which are crucial for proactive investigation. Option B (Amazon EventBridge) is essential for automating response workflows. Security Hub findings can be routed to EventBridge, which can then trigger AWS Lambda functions for automated remediation, send notifications, or integrate with incident response tools. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is for querying logs, which is part of investigation, but Security Hub and EventBridge are more focused on the overarching proactive response workflow. Option D (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is for analyzing CloudTrail events, a specific data source. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service, not directly for proactive investigation and response automation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_86',
  'A security architect is designing a solution to parse and normalize various log formats from different AWS services and custom applications. The goal is to prepare the data for unified analysis and correlation. Which two services are commonly used in conjunction to achieve log parsing and normalization before analysis? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Lambda","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon S3","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Security Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (AWS Lambda) can be used to process incoming log data (e.g., from Kinesis Data Firehose or S3 events) to parse, transform, and normalize it into a consistent format before it''s stored or sent for analysis. Option E (AWS Security Lake) automatically normalizes security data from various sources into the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) format, making it a direct solution for normalization of security logs. Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a delivery service that can stream data to destinations like S3 or OpenSearch Service, and it can perform some basic transformations, but for complex parsing and normalization, Lambda is often integrated. Option C (Amazon S3) is a storage destination for logs, but it does not perform parsing or normalization. Option D (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) collects and monitors logs, but typically relies on other services like Lambda for advanced parsing and normalization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_87',
  'A security team needs to monitor for specific patterns in CloudTrail logs, such as repeated failed login attempts or unauthorized API calls, and trigger alerts. They also want to visualize these trends over time. Which two services are best suited for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch metric filters","Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards","Amazon EventBridge rules","AWS Security Lake","AWS CloudTrail Lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch metric filters) can be applied to CloudTrail logs (after being sent to CloudWatch Logs) to identify specific patterns like failed login attempts. These filters create custom metrics that can then be used to trigger alarms. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards) allows for the visualization of these custom metrics over time, making it easy to spot trends and monitor for suspicious activities. Option C (Amazon EventBridge rules) are for automated responses to events, not primarily for creating metrics or visualizing trends. Option D (AWS Security Lake) aggregates and normalizes security data, but CloudWatch is more direct for metric-based alerting and visualization of log patterns. Option E (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is for immutable storage and analysis of CloudTrail events, but CloudWatch provides the real-time metric filtering and dashboarding capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_88',
  'A security engineer is designing a log analysis solution that requires the ability to identify anomalies in security logs and correlate events across different AWS services. They need a service that can process large volumes of data and provide insights. Which two services are commonly used together for anomaly detection and correlation in security logs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS Security Lake","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is a powerful analytics engine that excels at processing large volumes of log data, performing complex queries, and identifying anomalies through its search and aggregation capabilities. Option B (AWS Security Lake) centralizes and normalizes security data from various sources into the OCSF format, providing a unified dataset that is ideal for feeding into OpenSearch Service for correlation and anomaly detection. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) is excellent for querying CloudWatch Logs, but OpenSearch Service offers more advanced anomaly detection and correlation capabilities across diverse data sources. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is for event routing and automated responses, not for log analysis and anomaly detection. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a data delivery service, not an analysis service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_89',
  'Which two AWS services provide a SQL-like interface for querying and analyzing aggregated CloudTrail logs to identify suspicious API calls across multiple AWS accounts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights.","AWS CloudTrail Lake.","Amazon Athena.","Amazon OpenSearch Service.","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, which is purpose-built for querying and analyzing CloudTrail logs with a SQL-like interface, supporting advanced filtering and multi-account aggregation. Option C (correct) is Amazon Athena, which can query CloudTrail logs stored in Amazon S3 using standard SQL, making it suitable for identifying suspicious API calls across aggregated logs. Option A (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights, which queries logs stored in CloudWatch Logs, not directly aggregated CloudTrail logs in S3 or CloudTrail Lake. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon OpenSearch Service, which is a search and analytics engine that can be used for SIEM, but it requires data ingestion and is not primarily a SQL-like interface for direct S3/CloudTrail Lake querying. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, an ingestion service, not a query service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_90',
  'Which two AWS services provide capabilities for analyzing and visualizing log data, including the ability to identify anomalies and build dashboards? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights.","Amazon OpenSearch Service.","AWS CloudTrail Lake.","Amazon Athena.","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights, which allows for interactive analysis of log data within Amazon CloudWatch Logs, including identifying trends and patterns. While it has limited visualization, it''s strong on analysis. Option B (correct) is Amazon OpenSearch Service, which is a powerful search and analytics engine that provides SIEM capabilities, robust visualization with Kibana/OpenSearch Dashboards, and features for anomaly detection and building comprehensive dashboards. Option C (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, primarily for querying CloudTrail logs, not general log visualization or anomaly detection. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon Athena, a query service, but it does not natively provide anomaly detection or dashboarding capabilities. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, an ingestion service, not an analysis or visualization service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_91',
  'A large enterprise needs to centralize security data from various AWS accounts and normalize it into a standard format for easier analysis. Which AWS services can facilitate this aggregation and standardization? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Lake) is designed to centralize security data from cloud and on-premises sources into a purpose-built data lake, automatically normalizing it to the Open Cyber Security Schema Framework (OCSF) format. Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) can be used to stream raw security logs from various sources to a central Amazon S3 bucket, which can then be processed or ingested by Security Lake. Option C (Amazon EventBridge) is for routing events and triggering actions, not for data aggregation or normalization. Option D (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is for collecting and monitoring logs, but doesn''t inherently normalize data across different sources to OCSF. Option E (AWS Config) assesses and audits the configurations of your AWS resources, it does not aggregate security data in OCSF format.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_92',
  'Which AWS services are commonly used to build a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) solution on AWS, providing capabilities for log aggregation, analysis, and visualization? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS Security Lake","AWS Systems Manager","Amazon DynamoDB","AWS WAF"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is a managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch clusters, which are commonly used for SIEM solutions for log aggregation, search, and visualization. Option B (AWS Security Lake) can act as the centralized data lake for security logs, normalizing them to OCSF, which can then feed into a SIEM solution built on OpenSearch Service or other tools. Option C (AWS Systems Manager) is for operational insights and automation, not SIEM. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, not typically used as a core SIEM component for log analysis. Option E (AWS WAF) is a web application firewall, a source of security data, not a SIEM solution itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_55_m_55_5_93',
  'A company requires real-time processing and analysis of streaming security logs to detect threats quickly. Which AWS services can be leveraged to ingest and process these logs for immediate analysis? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS Lambda","Amazon S3 Glacier","Amazon Redshift","AWS Step Functions"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations like Amazon S3, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Amazon CloudWatch Logs, making it suitable for ingestion. Option B (AWS Lambda) can be invoked by streaming services like Kinesis to process logs in real-time, performing transformations, filtering, or sending alerts. Option C (Amazon S3 Glacier) is for archival storage, not real-time processing. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse for analytical queries, typically on batch data, not real-time streaming. Option E (AWS Step Functions) orchestrates serverless workflows, but is not an ingestion or real-time processing service itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain55_12',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_55_5',
  'medium'
);

