INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_100',
  'An administrator needs to create a resource-based policy to grant specific permissions to an Amazon S3 bucket. Order the general steps to define such a policy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the ''Action'' (what operations are allowed).","Attach the policy directly to the Amazon S3 bucket.","Define the ''Principal'' (who is allowed access).","Define the ''Resource'' (which S3 bucket is affected)."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'When creating a resource-based policy, the logical flow is to first define Option C (the ''Principal''), specifying who is granted access. Then, Option A (define the ''Action''), detailing what operations the principal can perform. Next, Option D (define the ''Resource''), indicating which specific Amazon S3 bucket the policy applies to. Finally, Option B (attach the policy directly to the Amazon S3 bucket) for it to take effect.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_101',
  'A development team in Account A needs to access specific resources in Account B. Order the high-level steps to establish secure cross-account access using an IAM role.',
  'ordering',
  '["Attach an IAM permissions policy to the role in Account B.","Create an IAM policy in Account A allowing ''sts:AssumeRole'' on the role in Account B.","Create an IAM role in Account B with a trust policy allowing principals from Account A.","Attach the ''sts:AssumeRole'' policy to the relevant IAM user or role in Account A."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'To set up cross-account access, first, Option C (create an IAM role in Account B with a trust policy allowing principals from Account A). Then, Option A (attach an IAM permissions policy to the role in Account B) to define what actions the assumed role can perform. Next, Option B (create an IAM policy in Account A allowing ''sts:AssumeRole'' on the role in Account B). Finally, Option D (attach the ''sts:AssumeRole'' policy to the relevant IAM user or role in Account A) to grant the principal the ability to assume the role.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_102',
  'A security team needs to implement preventive guardrails across multiple AWS accounts using AWS Organizations. Order the steps to deploy a basic Service Control Policy (SCP) strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable all features in AWS Organizations.","Create Organizational Units (OUs) to group accounts.","Attach Service Control Policies (SCPs) to OUs or individual accounts.","Create Service Control Policies (SCPs) with desired permissions."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'To implement an SCP strategy, first, Option A (enable all features in AWS Organizations) to allow SCPs. Then, Option B (create Organizational Units (OUs) to group accounts) for logical organization. Next, Option D (create Service Control Policies (SCPs) with desired permissions) that define the maximum permissions allowed. Finally, Option C (attach Service Control Policies (SCPs) to OUs or individual accounts) to enforce the guardrails.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_103',
  'A company wants to implement Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) to manage access to resources based on tags. Order the steps to configure ABAC for IAM principals and resources.',
  'ordering',
  '["Tag AWS resources with relevant attributes (e.g., ''Project:Alpha'').","Create an IAM policy using condition keys like ''aws:ResourceTag'' and ''aws:PrincipalTag''.","Tag IAM principals (users/roles) with matching attributes.","Attach the ABAC policy to the IAM principals."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'Implementing ABAC starts with Option A (tagging AWS resources with relevant attributes). Concurrently, Option C (tagging IAM principals (users/roles) with matching attributes). Once resources and principals are tagged, Option B (create an IAM policy using condition keys like ''aws:ResourceTag'' and ''aws:PrincipalTag'') to enforce access based on these tags. Finally, Option D (attach the ABAC policy to the IAM principals) to apply the access control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_104',
  'A company needs to grant a third-party vendor access to an Amazon S3 bucket in their AWS account, ensuring the vendor uses a unique identifier to prevent confused deputy attacks. Order the steps to securely configure this access using `sts:ExternalId`.',
  'ordering',
  '["The third-party vendor calls the AWS STS AssumeRole API operation with the provided ExternalId.","Create an IAM role in your AWS account with a trust policy that includes the `sts:ExternalId` condition.","Share the IAM role ARN and the unique `ExternalId` value with the third-party vendor.","The third-party vendor receives temporary security credentials to access the S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To securely grant third-party access with `sts:ExternalId`, first, Option B (create an IAM role in your AWS account with a trust policy that includes the `sts:ExternalId` condition). Then, Option C (share the IAM role ARN and the unique `ExternalId` value with the third-party vendor). Next, Option A (the third-party vendor calls the AWS STS AssumeRole API operation with the provided ExternalId). Finally, Option D (the third-party vendor receives temporary security credentials to access the S3 bucket).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_105',
  'Some AWS services require specific permissions to interact with other services on your behalf, which are granted through service-linked roles. Order the typical sequence of events when an AWS service creates and uses a service-linked role.',
  'ordering',
  '["An AWS service feature that requires a service-linked role is enabled or configured.","The AWS service automatically creates the service-linked role in your account.","The service assumes the service-linked role to perform actions on your behalf.","The service-linked role''s permissions can be reviewed in the IAM console."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'When an AWS service needs to perform actions on your behalf, it often uses a service-linked role. The process starts when Option A (an AWS service feature that requires a service-linked role is enabled or configured). Subsequently, Option B (the AWS service automatically creates the service-linked role in your account). Then, Option C (the service assumes the service-linked role to perform actions on your behalf) as needed. Finally, Option D (the service-linked role''s permissions can be reviewed in the IAM console) to understand its capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_106',
  'A developer reports an ''Access Denied'' error for an action that should be allowed according to their IAM policy. The organization uses AWS Organizations with SCPs. Order the steps to troubleshoot if an SCP is causing the denial.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review AWS CloudTrail logs for the denied action to identify the principal and resource.","Examine the SCPs attached to the account''s Organizational Unit (OU) hierarchy.","Use the IAM Policy Simulator to test the action against the effective permissions.","Modify or detach the denying SCP if it is unintentionally blocking the action."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'When troubleshooting an ''Access Denied'' error potentially caused by an SCP, start by Option A (reviewing AWS CloudTrail logs for the denied action to identify the principal and resource). Next, Option B (examine the SCPs attached to the account''s Organizational Unit (OU) hierarchy) to see if any SCP explicitly denies the action. Then, Option C (use the IAM Policy Simulator to test the action against the effective permissions) to confirm the denial source. Finally, Option D (modify or detach the denying SCP if it is unintentionally blocking the action) to resolve the issue.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_107',
  'An administrator needs to create a new IAM policy that adheres strictly to the principle of least privilege. Order the general steps to define such a policy effectively.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the specific AWS services and actions required for the task.","Identify the specific AWS resources that need to be accessed.","Write the IAM policy with ''Allow'' statements for only the identified actions and resources.","Test the policy using the IAM Policy Simulator to validate permissions."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To create an IAM policy following the principle of least privilege, first, Option A (identify the specific AWS services and actions required for the task). Next, Option B (identify the specific AWS resources that need to be accessed). Then, Option C (write the IAM policy with ''Allow'' statements for only the identified actions and resources), ensuring no unnecessary permissions are granted. Finally, Option D (test the policy using the IAM Policy Simulator to validate permissions) before deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_108',
  'A security administrator needs to create an IAM role in Account B that allows an IAM user in Account A to assume it for specific administrative tasks. Order the steps to correctly set up this cross-account role.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the permissions policy for the role, granting necessary actions.","Create the IAM role in Account B, specifying Account A''s ID as a trusted entity.","Attach the defined permissions policy to the newly created role.","Provide the role''s ARN to the IAM user in Account A for assumption."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To create a cross-account role, you first need to Option B (Create the IAM role in Account B, specifying Account A''s ID as a trusted entity) in the trust policy. Next, you Option A (Define the permissions policy for the role), outlining what actions the assumed role can perform. Then, Option C (Attach the defined permissions policy to the newly created role). Finally, Option D (Provide the role''s ARN to the IAM user in Account A) so they can use `sts:AssumeRole`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_109',
  'An AWS Organization has multiple Organizational Units (OUs) and accounts. A user in a nested account attempts an action. Order the sequence in which Service Control Policies (SCPs) are evaluated to determine if the action is allowed.',
  'ordering',
  '["SCPs attached to the root of the organization.","SCPs attached to the direct parent OU of the account.","SCPs attached to any higher-level OUs in the hierarchy.","SCPs attached directly to the individual AWS account."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'SCPs are inherited down the hierarchy. The evaluation starts from the top: Option A (SCPs attached to the root of the organization) are evaluated first. Then, Option C (SCPs attached to any higher-level OUs in the hierarchy) are evaluated from parent to child. Next, Option B (SCPs attached to the direct parent OU of the account) are evaluated. Finally, Option D (SCPs attached directly to the individual AWS account) are evaluated. An explicit deny at any level in this chain will prevent the action, regardless of allows at lower levels.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_110',
  'A company wants to implement Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) to manage access to resources based on project tags. Order the steps to set up ABAC for IAM principals and AWS resources.',
  'ordering',
  '["Attach the ABAC policies to the relevant IAM principals.","Tag AWS resources (e.g., EC2 instances, S3 buckets) with project-specific tags.","Create IAM policies that use condition keys like `aws:ResourceTag` and `aws:PrincipalTag`.","Tag IAM principals (users or roles) with corresponding project-specific tags."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'Implementing ABAC starts with tagging. First, Option B (Tag AWS resources with project-specific tags) to categorize them. Then, Option D (Tag IAM principals with corresponding project-specific tags) to identify who can access what. Next, Option C (Create IAM policies that use condition keys like `aws:ResourceTag` and `aws:PrincipalTag`) to define the ABAC rules. Finally, Option A (Attach the ABAC policies to the relevant IAM principals) to enforce the access control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_111',
  'A security auditor needs to understand the permissions granted to a service-linked role in an AWS account. Order the steps to effectively review the permissions of a service-linked role.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the specific service-linked role in IAM.","Review the managed policies automatically attached to the role.","Understand the AWS service''s requirements for the role.","Check for any inline policies that might be attached to the role."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To understand service-linked role permissions, first Option A (Identify the specific service-linked role in IAM). Then, Option C (Understand the AWS service''s requirements for the role) to grasp its intended purpose. Next, Option B (Review the managed policies automatically attached to the role), as these define the core permissions. Finally, Option D (Check for any inline policies that might be attached to the role), although service-linked roles typically rely on managed policies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_112',
  'A security team needs to prevent any account within a specific Organizational Unit (OU) from launching Amazon EC2 instances in unsupported AWS Regions. Order the steps to implement this preventive control using Service Control Policies (SCPs).',
  'ordering',
  '["Test the SCP by attempting to launch an EC2 instance in an unsupported Region from an affected account.","Create a new Service Control Policy (SCP) that explicitly denies `ec2:RunInstances` in the unsupported Regions.","Attach the newly created SCP to the target Organizational Unit (OU).","Ensure the SCP does not conflict with or is not overridden by higher-level SCPs."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'To implement preventive controls with SCPs, first Option B (Create a new Service Control Policy (SCP) that explicitly denies `ec2:RunInstances` in the unsupported Regions). Then, Option C (Attach the newly created SCP to the target Organizational Unit (OU)) to apply it to all accounts within that OU. Next, Option D (Ensure the SCP does not conflict with or is not overridden by higher-level SCPs) as explicit denies at higher levels can block the desired behavior. Finally, Option A (Test the SCP by attempting to launch an EC2 instance in an unsupported Region) to confirm its effectiveness.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_113',
  'A company wants to implement Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) to allow developers access to specific Amazon S3 buckets only if their IAM role and the S3 bucket share a common ''Project'' tag. Order the steps to configure this ABAC solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an IAM policy that uses `aws:PrincipalTag/Project` and `aws:ResourceTag/Project` condition keys.","Tag the developers'' IAM roles with a ''Project'' tag (e.g., ''Project'': ''Alpha'').","Attach the ABAC policy to the developers'' IAM roles.","Tag the Amazon S3 buckets with the corresponding ''Project'' tag (e.g., ''Project'': ''Alpha'')."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'ABAC relies on consistent tagging. First, Option D (Tag the Amazon S3 buckets with the corresponding ''Project'' tag). Then, Option B (Tag the developers'' IAM roles with a ''Project'' tag) to match the resources. Next, Option A (Create an IAM policy that uses `aws:PrincipalTag/Project` and `aws:ResourceTag/Project` condition keys) to define the access rule. Finally, Option C (Attach the ABAC policy to the developers'' IAM roles) to enforce the tag-based access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_114',
  'A security architect needs to grant cross-account read-only access to a specific Amazon S3 bucket for an IAM role in another AWS account. Order the steps to achieve this using a resource-based policy on the S3 bucket.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the specific S3 permissions (e.g., `s3:GetObject`) to be granted to the principal.","Create an Amazon S3 bucket policy document.","Specify the ARN of the IAM role from the other account as the `Principal` in the policy.","Apply the completed bucket policy to the target Amazon S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To grant cross-account access via a resource policy, first Option B (Create an Amazon S3 bucket policy document). Within this document, Option C (Specify the ARN of the IAM role from the other account as the `Principal`) to identify who is allowed. Next, Option A (Define the specific S3 permissions) that the principal can perform. Finally, Option D (Apply the completed bucket policy to the target Amazon S3 bucket) to enforce the access control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_115',
  'A large organization uses AWS Organizations to manage its accounts and wants to implement Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) for Amazon S3 buckets. The goal is to allow developers to access S3 buckets only if the bucket is tagged with their project ID and the developer''s IAM role also has a matching project tag. This approach minimizes the need for individual policies per project. Order the steps to implement ABAC for S3 bucket access.',
  'ordering',
  '["Tag the Amazon S3 buckets with `Project: <ProjectID>`.","Create an IAM policy that uses condition keys to allow S3 actions only when the principal''s `aws:PrincipalTag/Project` matches the resource''s `s3:ResourceTag/Project`.","Attach the ABAC-enabled IAM policy to developer IAM roles.","Tag the developer IAM roles with `Project: <ProjectID>`."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'To implement Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) for Amazon S3 bucket access, the steps are: First, create an IAM policy that leverages condition keys to allow S3 actions only when the principal''s `aws:PrincipalTag/Project` matches the resource''s `s3:ResourceTag/Project` (Option B). This policy defines the ABAC logic. Next, attach this ABAC-enabled IAM policy to the developer IAM roles (Option C). Then, tag the developer IAM roles with `Project: <ProjectID>` (Option D) to define their project affiliation. Finally, tag the Amazon S3 buckets with `Project: <ProjectID>` (Option A) to associate them with specific projects. This order ensures the policy is defined and attached before the tags are applied, making the access control effective.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_116',
  'A company needs to grant a trusted third-party vendor programmatic access to an Amazon DynamoDB table in their production AWS account. The access must be read-only and limited to specific items based on a partition key. The security team insists on using an `sts:ExternalId` to prevent confused deputy attacks. Order the steps to securely configure this cross-account access for the third-party vendor.',
  'ordering',
  '["Provide the third-party vendor with the ARN of the IAM role and the `ExternalId` value.","Create an IAM role in the production AWS account with a trust policy that specifies the third-party vendor''s AWS account ID and an `sts:ExternalId` condition.","Attach an identity-based policy to the IAM role, granting read-only permissions to the DynamoDB table, with a condition to restrict access by partition key.","The third-party vendor configures their application to assume the IAM role using `sts:AssumeRole` with the provided `ExternalId`."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To securely configure cross-account access for a third-party vendor with an `sts:ExternalId`, the steps are: First, create an IAM role in the production AWS account. Its trust policy must explicitly allow the third-party vendor''s AWS account ID to assume it and include a strong `sts:ExternalId` condition to prevent confused deputy attacks (Option B). Next, attach an identity-based policy to this IAM role, granting specific read-only permissions to the DynamoDB table and including a condition to restrict access by partition key for granular control (Option C). Then, securely provide the third-party vendor with the ARN of this IAM role and the `ExternalId` value (Option A). Finally, the third-party vendor will configure their application to assume the IAM role using the `sts:AssumeRole` API call, including the provided `ExternalId` in their request (Option D). This aligns with anti-pattern ap-4.2-001 by using condition keys for cross-account access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_117',
  'A security team in a multi-account AWS Organization wants to enforce a preventive control that prevents any Amazon S3 bucket in any member account from being made publicly accessible. This is a critical security requirement to avoid data breaches. Order the steps to implement this control using an AWS Organizations Service Control Policy (SCP).',
  'ordering',
  '["Attach the SCP to the Organizational Unit (OU) or directly to the member accounts where the restriction needs to apply.","Test the SCP by attempting to create a public S3 bucket policy in a member account and verifying the denial.","Create a Service Control Policy (SCP) in the AWS Organizations management account that explicitly denies `s3:PutBucketPolicy` and `s3:PutBucketAcl` actions when the `s3:x-amz-acl` condition is ''public-read'' or ''public-read-write'', or when a bucket policy grants public access.","Ensure that the SCP does not inadvertently block legitimate S3 operations that are not related to public access."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'To implement a preventive control preventing public S3 buckets using an SCP, the steps are: First, create a Service Control Policy (SCP) in the AWS Organizations management account. This SCP should explicitly deny actions like `s3:PutBucketPolicy` and `s3:PutBucketAcl` when conditions indicate public access (e.g., `s3:x-amz-acl` is ''public-read'' or ''public-read-write'', or the policy grants public access) (Option C). Next, attach this SCP to the relevant Organizational Unit (OU) or directly to the member accounts where the restriction needs to apply (Option A). After attachment, ensure that the SCP does not inadvertently block legitimate S3 operations that are not related to public access (Option D), which might require careful refinement of the SCP conditions. Finally, it is crucial to test the SCP by attempting to create a public S3 bucket policy in a member account and verifying that the action is denied as expected (Option B).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_118',
  'A new AWS service, AWS Backup, is being rolled out across several member accounts in an AWS Organization. The security team needs to ensure that AWS Backup has the necessary permissions to perform its functions (e.g., creating snapshots, restoring data) within each account, but without granting excessive privileges. AWS Backup uses a service-linked role. Order the steps to enable and manage this service-linked role effectively.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify the default permissions of the `AWSServiceRoleForBackup` service-linked role to understand its capabilities.","Enable AWS Backup in each member account where it will be used, which automatically creates the `AWSServiceRoleForBackup` service-linked role.","Monitor CloudTrail logs for actions taken by the `AWSServiceRoleForBackup` to ensure it operates as expected and does not exhibit unusual activity.","Review the service-linked role''s permissions regularly using IAM Access Analyzer to identify any potential over-privileges or unused permissions."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'To effectively enable and manage a service-linked role like `AWSServiceRoleForBackup`, the steps are: First, enable AWS Backup in each member account where it will be used. This action automatically creates the `AWSServiceRoleForBackup` service-linked role with predefined permissions (Option B). Next, verify the default permissions of the `AWSServiceRoleForBackup` service-linked role to understand its capabilities and ensure it aligns with the principle of least privilege (Option A). Then, regularly review the service-linked role''s permissions using IAM Access Analyzer to identify any potential over-privileges or unused permissions that could be refined (Option D). Finally, monitor CloudTrail logs for actions taken by the `AWSServiceRoleForBackup` to ensure it operates as expected and does not exhibit unusual or unauthorized activity (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_119',
  'A company is migrating its application from a single AWS account to a multi-account architecture managed by AWS Organizations. They have an Amazon SQS queue in a central logging account that needs to receive messages from an application in a separate development account. The security team requires a secure cross-account authorization mechanism using a resource-based policy. Order the steps to configure this cross-account access for the SQS queue.',
  'ordering',
  '["In the central logging account, attach a resource-based policy to the Amazon SQS queue that allows `sqs:SendMessage` from the development account''s AWS account ID.","In the development account, configure the application''s IAM role to have `sqs:SendMessage` permissions to the specific SQS queue ARN.","Identify the ARN of the Amazon SQS queue in the central logging account and the AWS account ID of the development account.","Test the cross-account message sending by deploying the application in the development account and verifying messages arrive in the SQS queue."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'To configure cross-account access for an Amazon SQS queue using a resource-based policy, the steps are: First, identify the ARN of the Amazon SQS queue in the central logging account and the AWS account ID of the development account (Option C). These identifiers are crucial for policy configuration. Next, in the central logging account, attach a resource-based policy directly to the Amazon SQS queue. This policy should explicitly allow the `sqs:SendMessage` action from the development account''s AWS account ID (Option A). Then, in the development account, configure the application''s IAM role (which the application assumes) to have `sqs:SendMessage` permissions to the specific SQS queue ARN (Option B). This ensures the application has the necessary identity-based permissions. Finally, test the cross-account message sending by deploying the application in the development account and verifying that messages successfully arrive in the SQS queue (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_120',
  'A security engineer is tasked with refining an existing broad IAM policy that grants wide access to Amazon EC2 instances. The goal is to implement a more granular authorization model using Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) to restrict access based on instance tags (e.g., ''Environment: Dev'', ''Owner: JohnDoe'') and the user''s corresponding principal tags. Order the steps to transition from a broad policy to an ABAC-based policy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the IAM policy to include condition keys that compare the principal''s tags (`aws:PrincipalTag`) with the resource''s tags (`ec2:ResourceTag`) for EC2 actions.","Tag all existing and new Amazon EC2 instances with appropriate key-value pairs (e.g., `Environment: Dev`).","Tag the IAM users or roles that need to access EC2 instances with corresponding principal tags (e.g., `Owner: JohnDoe`).","Test the refined ABAC policy using IAM Policy Simulator to ensure proper access control and identify any unintended denials."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To transition from a broad IAM policy to an ABAC-based policy for Amazon EC2 instances, the steps are: First, tag all existing and new Amazon EC2 instances with appropriate key-value pairs (e.g., `Environment: Dev`) (Option B). This establishes the resource attributes for ABAC. Next, tag the IAM users or roles that need to access EC2 instances with corresponding principal tags (e.g., `Owner: JohnDoe`) (Option C). These are the principal attributes. Then, update the existing broad IAM policy to include condition keys that compare the principal''s tags (`aws:PrincipalTag`) with the resource''s tags (`ec2:ResourceTag`) for EC2 actions, thereby enforcing ABAC (Option A). Finally, test the refined ABAC policy using IAM Policy Simulator to ensure proper access control and identify any unintended denials before full deployment (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_121',
  'A company has a multi-account strategy where a central security account manages all security-related configurations and audits. They need to grant an IAM role in the security account read-only access to Amazon CloudWatch logs in all development accounts within the AWS Organization. The solution must be scalable and prevent individual policy updates for each new development account. Order the steps to achieve this using cross-account role assumption with `aws:PrincipalOrgID`.',
  'ordering',
  '["In each development account, create an IAM role (e.g., `SecurityAuditRole`) with a trust policy that allows the security account''s AWS account ID to assume it, and include an `aws:PrincipalOrgID` condition.","In the security account, create an IAM role for auditors with an identity-based policy allowing `sts:AssumeRole` on the `SecurityAuditRole` in all development accounts.","Attach an identity-based policy to the `SecurityAuditRole` in each development account, granting read-only permissions to CloudWatch Logs.","Auditors in the security account assume their IAM role, then assume the `SecurityAuditRole` in a target development account to access CloudWatch Logs."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To achieve scalable cross-account read-only access to CloudWatch Logs using `aws:PrincipalOrgID`, the steps are: First, in each development account, create an IAM role (e.g., `SecurityAuditRole`). Its trust policy should allow the security account''s AWS account ID to assume it, and critically, include an `aws:PrincipalOrgID` condition to restrict assumption to principals within the organization (Option A). Next, attach an identity-based policy to this `SecurityAuditRole` in each development account, granting read-only permissions to CloudWatch Logs (Option C). Then, in the central security account, create an IAM role for the auditors. This role will have an identity-based policy allowing `sts:AssumeRole` on the `SecurityAuditRole` in all development accounts (Option B). Finally, auditors in the security account will first assume their IAM role, and then assume the `SecurityAuditRole` in a target development account to access CloudWatch Logs (Option D). This approach uses `aws:PrincipalOrgID` to prevent anti-pattern ap-4.2-001.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_122',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new application that uses Amazon ECS with Fargate. The application needs to interact with other AWS services, and the security team wants to ensure that the ECS tasks have the necessary permissions through a task execution role and a task role. Order the steps to correctly configure the IAM roles for an Amazon ECS Fargate task.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an IAM task role with permissions required by the application code running in the ECS task (e.g., access to Amazon S3, DynamoDB).","Create an IAM task execution role with permissions for ECS to pull container images from Amazon ECR and publish logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.","Specify both the task execution role and the task role in the Amazon ECS task definition.","Launch the Amazon ECS service or run a standalone task using the defined task definition."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To correctly configure IAM roles for an Amazon ECS Fargate task, the steps are: First, create an IAM task execution role. This role grants permissions to the Amazon ECS agent to perform actions like pulling container images from Amazon ECR and publishing logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs (Option B). Next, create an IAM task role. This role grants permissions specifically required by the application code running inside the ECS container (e.g., access to Amazon S3, DynamoDB) (Option A). Then, specify both the task execution role and the task role in the Amazon ECS task definition (Option C). This links the roles to the task. Finally, launch the Amazon ECS service or run a standalone task using the defined task definition, which will then assume these roles (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_123',
  'A company is setting up a new AWS account for a sensitive data analytics project. The security team wants to ensure that all data stored in Amazon S3 buckets within this account is encrypted by default and that only specific IAM roles can decrypt it. They also want to prevent any IAM user or role from disabling default encryption. Order the steps to implement these security controls for S3 buckets in the new account.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an IAM policy that allows only specific IAM roles to use the KMS key for decryption and prevents disabling default encryption.","Configure the default encryption for all new S3 buckets in the account to use the KMS key.","Create an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) Customer Managed Key (CMK) for S3 encryption.","Attach the IAM policy to the KMS key as a key policy, explicitly denying actions that would disable encryption or grant unauthorized decryption."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'To implement robust encryption controls for S3 buckets in a new account, the steps are: First, create an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) Customer Managed Key (CMK) that will be used for S3 encryption (Option C). This provides granular control over the encryption key. Next, create an IAM policy that allows only specific IAM roles to use this KMS key for decryption and explicitly prevents any action that would disable default encryption (Option A). Then, attach this IAM policy to the KMS key as a key policy. Key policies are fundamental for controlling access to the KMS key itself (Option D). Finally, configure the default encryption for all new S3 buckets in the account to use this KMS key (Option B). This ensures all new objects are encrypted with the controlled key.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_57_m_57_2_124',
  'A security team needs to establish a clear separation of duties for managing IAM policies within a development AWS account. They want to ensure that one group of administrators can create and attach IAM policies, but another group is responsible for reviewing and approving these policies before they become effective. Order the steps to implement a workflow that supports this separation of duties.',
  'ordering',
  '["Grant the ''Policy Approvers'' group permissions to review and attach IAM policies to principals.","Grant the ''Policy Creators'' group permissions to create and manage IAM policies, but not attach them.","Implement a manual or automated review process where ''Policy Approvers'' examine policies created by ''Policy Creators''.","Configure AWS CloudTrail to log all IAM policy creation and attachment events for auditing."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To implement a workflow that supports separation of duties for IAM policy management, the steps are: First, grant the ''Policy Creators'' group permissions to create and manage IAM policies, but explicitly deny them permissions to attach these policies to principals (Option B). This ensures they can draft policies. Next, grant the ''Policy Approvers'' group permissions to review policies and attach them to principals (Option A). This separates the creation from the approval/attachment. Then, implement a manual or automated review process where ''Policy Approvers'' examine policies created by ''Policy Creators'' before attachment (Option C). Finally, configure AWS CloudTrail to log all IAM policy creation and attachment events for auditing purposes, providing an immutable record of changes (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain57_12',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_57_2',
  'hard'
);

