  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_35',
  'What type of AWS KMS key allows customers to import their own key material and retain full control over its lifecycle within AWS KMS?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS managed key","Customer managed key","AWS owned key","CloudHSM key"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Customer managed key) is the type of AWS KMS key that allows customers to import their own key material and retain full control over its lifecycle, including key policies, rotation, and deletion scheduling. Option A (AWS managed key) is created and managed by AWS on the customer''s behalf. Option C (AWS owned key) is a collection of KMS keys that AWS owns and manages for use in multiple AWS services. Option D (CloudHSM key) refers to keys managed within AWS CloudHSM, which is a different service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_36',
  'A company needs to store non-sensitive application logs in Amazon S3 and wants to ensure they are encrypted at rest with the LEAST operational overhead. Which Amazon S3 encryption option should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) keys (SSE-KMS)","Customer-provided encryption keys (SSE-C)","Dual-layer server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (DSSE-KMS)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)) is the correct choice for encrypting data at rest in Amazon S3 with the least operational overhead. SSE-S3 requires no additional key management from the customer, as AWS handles the keys entirely. Option B (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) keys (SSE-KMS)) provides more control and auditability over the encryption keys but introduces the overhead of managing KMS keys and policies. Option C (Customer-provided encryption keys (SSE-C)) requires the customer to manage and provide their own encryption keys for every request, which significantly increases operational complexity. Option D (Dual-layer server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (DSSE-KMS)) offers enhanced security by applying two layers of encryption but also increases operational overhead compared to SSE-S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_37',
  'Which AWS service provides a dedicated Hardware Security Module (HSM) in the cloud that is FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated, allowing customers to maintain full control over their encryption keys?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS CloudHSM","Amazon Macie"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS CloudHSM) is the correct service. AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated, single-tenant Hardware Security Modules (HSMs) in the AWS cloud, which are FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated. This allows customers to have exclusive control over their encryption keys within a secure hardware environment. Option A (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) uses FIPS 140-2 Level 2 validated HSMs, but the HSMs are shared and managed by AWS. Option B (AWS Secrets Manager) is used for managing and rotating secrets, not for providing FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated HSMs. Option D (Amazon Macie) is a data security and privacy service that discovers and protects sensitive data, not an HSM service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_38',
  'A database administrator needs to ensure that all new and existing Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instances are encrypted at rest. The company requires using customer-managed keys for this encryption. Which approach should the administrator take?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable Amazon S3 default encryption for the RDS instance backups.","Configure encryption using AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for the RDS instances.","Create new encrypted RDS instances and restore existing data from unencrypted snapshots into encrypted ones.","Apply an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policy to the RDS service role to enforce encryption."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Create new encrypted RDS instances and restore existing data from unencrypted snapshots into encrypted ones) is the correct approach. To encrypt an existing unencrypted Amazon RDS instance, you must create a snapshot of the unencrypted instance, then copy the snapshot and enable encryption during the copy process using an AWS KMS key. Finally, restore a new RDS instance from this encrypted snapshot. Option A (Enable Amazon S3 default encryption for the RDS instance backups) only encrypts the backups, not the primary database instance. Option B (Configure encryption using AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for the RDS instances) is incorrect; ACM is for managing SSL/TLS certificates for data in transit, not for encrypting data at rest in RDS. Option D (Apply an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policy to the RDS service role to enforce encryption) is insufficient; while IAM policies control permissions, they do not directly encrypt existing unencrypted RDS instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_39',
  'A financial institution needs to store audit logs in Amazon S3 for 7 years, ensuring that the objects cannot be altered or deleted by anyone, including the root user, to comply with regulatory requirements. Which Amazon S3 feature provides this Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) capability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Object Lock","Amazon S3 Versioning","Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies","Amazon S3 Block Public Access"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3 Object Lock) is the correct feature for this requirement. S3 Object Lock provides Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) protection, preventing objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely. This is crucial for regulatory compliance. Option B (Amazon S3 Versioning) keeps multiple versions of an object but does not prevent deletion or overwriting of specific versions. Option C (Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies) automate the transition or expiration of objects but do not provide WORM protection against deletion. Option D (Amazon S3 Block Public Access) prevents public access to buckets and objects but does not enforce WORM compliance for authenticated users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_40',
  'A company stores highly sensitive customer data in Amazon S3 that is subject to strict compliance regulations requiring detailed audit trails of key usage. Which Amazon S3 encryption option, combined with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS), should be implemented to meet this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)","Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer managed key","Server-Side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C)","Client-Side Encryption with a client-managed key"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer managed key) is the correct choice. SSE-KMS, when configured with a customer managed key, integrates with AWS CloudTrail to provide detailed logs of all cryptographic operations performed with the key. This allows for comprehensive auditing of key usage, which is a common requirement for compliance. Option A (Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)) uses AWS-managed keys, which do not provide customer-controlled key policies or detailed CloudTrail audit logs of key usage, making it unsuitable for strict compliance. Option C (Server-Side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C)) requires the customer to manage and provide keys, but AWS does not log the use of these keys in CloudTrail. Option D (Client-Side Encryption with a client-managed key) encrypts data before it leaves the client, but managing audit trails for client-managed keys can be complex and may not integrate seamlessly with AWS auditing tools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_41',
  'A security architect wants to ensure that all AWS KMS customer managed keys (CMKs) are automatically rotated annually to meet compliance requirements. Which AWS KMS feature should be enabled for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manual key rotation via AWS Lambda","Scheduled key deletion","Automatic key rotation for customer managed keys","Key policy enforcement"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Automatic key rotation for customer managed keys) is the correct AWS KMS feature. AWS KMS provides an option to enable automatic key rotation for customer managed keys, which rotates the cryptographic material of the key annually. Option A (Manual key rotation via AWS Lambda) is a custom solution that would require significant operational overhead to build and maintain. Option B (Scheduled key deletion) is for deleting a key entirely, not for rotating its cryptographic material. Option D (Key policy enforcement) controls access to the key but does not manage its rotation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_42',
  'A company has several Amazon EC2 instances with unencrypted Amazon EBS volumes. The security team mandates that all EBS volumes must be encrypted at rest using customer-managed keys. What is the MOST efficient way to encrypt these existing unencrypted EBS volumes?',
  'mcq',
  '["Modify the EBS volume attributes directly to enable encryption.","Detach the unencrypted volumes, create snapshots, copy the snapshots with encryption, and then create new encrypted volumes from the copied snapshots.","Use AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) to encrypt the EBS volumes.","Apply an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policy to the EC2 instance to enforce EBS encryption."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Detach the unencrypted volumes, create snapshots, copy the snapshots with encryption, and then create new encrypted volumes from the copied snapshots) is the most efficient and standard way to encrypt existing unencrypted Amazon EBS volumes. You cannot directly encrypt an existing unencrypted volume. The process involves creating an encrypted copy of the volume''s data. Option A (Modify the EBS volume attributes directly to enable encryption) is incorrect because EBS volumes cannot be encrypted in-place after creation. Option C (Use AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) to encrypt the EBS volumes) is incorrect; ACM is for managing SSL/TLS certificates for data in transit, not for encrypting EBS volumes at rest. Option D (Apply an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policy to the EC2 instance to enforce EBS encryption) is insufficient; while IAM policies control permissions, they do not directly encrypt existing unencrypted EBS volumes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_43',
  'Which Amazon S3 server-side encryption option requires the customer to manage and provide their own encryption keys with each API request?',
  'mcq',
  '["Server-Side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C)","Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)","Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS)","Dual-layer server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (DSSE-KMS)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Server-Side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C)) is the correct option. With SSE-C, Amazon S3 performs the encryption and decryption, but the customer is responsible for managing and providing the encryption keys as part of the API requests. Option B (Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)) uses keys entirely managed by AWS. Option C (Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS)) uses keys managed by AWS KMS, which can be customer managed keys but are not provided with every API request. Option D (Dual-layer server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (DSSE-KMS)) also uses AWS KMS keys and does not require the customer to provide keys with each request.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_44',
  'A global financial institution is migrating its highly sensitive customer transaction data to Amazon S3. The data is subject to stringent PCI DSS compliance requirements, which mandate detailed audit trails for all cryptographic key usage and customer control over key policies. The security team needs to implement a solution that provides encryption at rest for S3 objects, ensuring full compliance and auditability with minimal operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement S3 Default Encryption with Server-Side Encryption with AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) using a Customer Managed Key (CMK).","Enable S3 Default Encryption with Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3).","Utilize client-side encryption, where the application encrypts data before uploading to S3, managing keys on-premises.","Configure S3 Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys (SSE-C) and manage encryption keys within the application."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. SSE-KMS with a Customer Managed Key (CMK) allows the financial institution to maintain full control over the key policy, enabling granular permissions and providing detailed audit trails of all key usage via AWS CloudTrail, which is crucial for PCI DSS compliance. It also integrates seamlessly with S3, minimizing operational overhead compared to client-side encryption. Option B (incorrect) uses AWS-managed keys (SSE-S3), which do not provide customer-controlled key policies or detailed key usage audit trails in CloudTrail, failing to meet the PCI DSS compliance requirement for key auditability. This represents anti-pattern ap-5.1-001. Option C (incorrect) shifts the entire key management burden to the customer''s application and on-premises infrastructure, significantly increasing operational overhead and complexity, and potentially introducing new compliance challenges. Option D (incorrect) also places the burden of key management and rotation on the customer''s application, and while it uses customer-provided keys, it lacks the integrated auditability and centralized management benefits of AWS KMS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_45',
  'A government contractor is developing a new application that processes top-secret information. This application requires cryptographic keys to be generated, stored, and used within a FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated hardware security module (HSM) environment. The contractor needs a fully managed AWS service that meets this stringent requirement while integrating with AWS for key management operations.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize AWS CloudHSM to create a dedicated HSM cluster for key generation and storage.","Use AWS Key Management Service (KMS) with Customer Managed Keys (CMKs) for key management.","Store cryptographic keys in AWS Secrets Manager and manage their lifecycle.","Implement client-side encryption with keys managed in a self-managed HSM on an Amazon EC2 instance."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the only AWS service that provides FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated hardware security modules (HSMs) under customer control, which is a specific requirement for highly sensitive government workloads. AWS CloudHSM allows customers to generate and manage their own encryption keys within a dedicated, single-tenant HSM cluster. Option B (incorrect) provides FIPS 140-2 Level 2 validated HSMs for its Customer Managed Keys (CMKs), which does not meet the Level 3 requirement. While KMS is a powerful key management service, it doesn''t offer the same level of customer control over the underlying HSMs as CloudHSM. Option C (incorrect) is designed for managing secrets like database credentials and API keys, not for generating and storing cryptographic key material within FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated HSMs. Option D (incorrect) would involve significant operational overhead for managing the HSM, its software, and its integration on an EC2 instance, and it would not be a fully managed service as requested.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_46',
  'A healthcare provider needs to store patient medical records in Amazon S3. Due to strict regulatory compliance (e.g., HIPAA), these records must be immutable, meaning they cannot be altered or deleted for a period of 7 years, even by privileged users. The solution must also protect against ransomware attacks by preventing unauthorized modifications. The company seeks the most effective AWS S3 feature to enforce this Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) requirement.',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode with a default retention period of 7 years.","Activate S3 Versioning on the bucket and enable MFA Delete.","Implement S3 Lifecycle policies to prevent deletion for 7 years.","Utilize Amazon Glacier Vault Lock for the S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution. S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode enforces a WORM model, preventing objects from being overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user, until the retention period expires. This directly addresses the immutability and ransomware protection requirements for regulatory compliance. Option B (incorrect) protects against accidental deletion and overwrites by keeping previous versions, and MFA Delete adds an extra layer of security, but it does not prevent a privileged user from explicitly deleting all versions or disabling versioning, thus failing the strict immutability requirement. Option C (incorrect) is used for automating object transitions and expirations, not for enforcing WORM immutability. It can be configured to delete objects after a period, but not to prevent deletion for a specific period in an immutable manner. Option D (incorrect) is a feature for Amazon S3 Glacier vaults, not directly for S3 Standard buckets where active patient records would typically reside. While it provides WORM, S3 Object Lock is the direct solution for S3 buckets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_47',
  'An e-commerce platform uses an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database to store sensitive customer data, including payment information. The company''s security policy dictates that all data at rest must be encrypted using customer-managed keys for enhanced control and auditability. The solution must integrate seamlessly with RDS and minimize administrative overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable encryption for the Amazon RDS instance using an AWS Key Management Service (KMS) Customer Managed Key (CMK).","Encrypt the underlying Amazon EBS volumes attached to the Amazon RDS instance using AWS KMS.","Implement client-side encryption within the application before data is written to the Amazon RDS database.","Configure AWS CloudHSM to manage the encryption keys for the Amazon RDS database."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective and integrated solution. Amazon RDS provides native encryption at rest using AWS KMS. By selecting a Customer Managed Key (CMK), the company gains control over the encryption key and its policy, fulfilling the requirement for customer-managed keys with minimal administrative overhead. Option B (incorrect) is not the correct approach for RDS. When RDS encryption is enabled, it automatically encrypts the underlying EBS volumes, database snapshots, and automated backups. Manually encrypting EBS volumes is not how RDS encryption is managed. Option C (incorrect) would introduce significant complexity and operational overhead, as the application would need to manage encryption/decryption, key management, and performance implications, which is largely redundant given RDS''s native encryption capabilities. Option D (incorrect) is not directly supported for RDS database encryption. While CloudHSM provides FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated HSMs, RDS integrates with AWS KMS for its encryption at rest, which can use CMKs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_48',
  'A large enterprise utilizes a shared services AWS account to manage all cryptographic keys for various applications across multiple application accounts. A specific application in an application account needs to encrypt and decrypt data stored in Amazon S3 using a Customer Managed Key (CMK) residing in the shared services account. The security architect must ensure that the application''s IAM role has the necessary permissions to use this CMK, adhering to the principle of least privilege.',
  'mcq',
  '["Update the KMS key policy in the shared services account to grant `kms:Encrypt` and `kms:Decrypt` permissions to the application account''s specific IAM role.","Attach an IAM policy to the application account''s IAM role that grants `kms:Encrypt` and `kms:Decrypt` permissions on the CMK ARN.","Create an AWS KMS grant in the shared services account, giving the application account''s IAM role temporary access to the CMK.","Share the AWS KMS Customer Managed Key directly with the application account via AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the correct and most secure approach. For cross-account access to a KMS Customer Managed Key, both the resource-based policy (KMS key policy) and the identity-based policy (IAM policy attached to the IAM role) must explicitly allow the action. The key policy in the shared services account must grant the necessary permissions to the specific IAM role in the application account. Option B (incorrect) is insufficient on its own. While an IAM policy is required, it must be combined with the KMS key policy. An IAM policy alone cannot override a restrictive key policy. Option C (incorrect) can be used for temporary or more granular permissions, but for general cross-account usage, updating the key policy is the primary and most straightforward method. Grants are typically used for more dynamic or time-limited scenarios. Option D (incorrect) is not a valid operation for sharing KMS CMKs. AWS KMS keys cannot be shared directly via AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) in the same way other resources like VPC subnets or Transit Gateways can.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_49',
  'A research institution stores highly sensitive genomic data on an Amazon EFS file system, which is accessed by multiple Amazon EC2 instances. Due to strict data privacy regulations, all data stored on EFS must be encrypted at rest using customer-managed keys. The security team needs to implement this encryption with minimal impact on application performance and administrative overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the Amazon EFS file system to encrypt data at rest using an AWS Key Management Service (KMS) Customer Managed Key (CMK).","Implement client-side encryption within the applications running on EC2 instances before writing data to EFS.","Use the default Amazon EFS encryption, which encrypts data at rest with AWS-managed keys.","Encrypt the Amazon EBS volumes attached to the EC2 instances that mount the EFS file system."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective and integrated solution. Amazon EFS supports encryption of data at rest using AWS KMS. By specifying a Customer Managed Key (CMK), the institution gains control over the encryption key, meeting the requirement for customer-managed keys with minimal administrative overhead and without impacting application performance. Option B (incorrect) would introduce significant complexity, requiring every application to handle encryption/decryption and key management, leading to increased development and operational overhead, and potential performance degradation. Option C (incorrect) uses AWS-managed keys, which do not meet the requirement for customer-managed keys, as the customer would not have control over the key policy or audit trails. Option D (incorrect) is irrelevant for EFS encryption. EFS is a shared file system, and its encryption is configured at the file system level, not on the individual EBS volumes of the EC2 instances that mount it. EBS encryption is for block storage attached directly to an EC2 instance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_50',
  'A security team in a large enterprise is responsible for managing hundreds of AWS Key Management Service (KMS) Customer Managed Keys (CMKs). To comply with internal security policies and industry best practices, all CMKs used for encrypting sensitive data must be rotated annually. The team needs an efficient and automated way to ensure this rotation occurs without manual intervention or data re-encryption.',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable automatic key rotation for all AWS KMS Customer Managed Keys (CMKs).","Manually create new CMKs annually and re-encrypt all data with the new keys.","Develop a custom script to periodically generate new key material and import it into existing CMKs.","Switch to using AWS-managed keys (AWS owned or AWS managed CMKs) which rotate automatically."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most efficient and automated solution. AWS KMS provides a built-in feature to enable automatic key rotation for Customer Managed Keys (CMKs). When enabled, KMS automatically rotates the cryptographic material for the CMK every year (approximately 365 days) without requiring any data re-encryption or application changes. Option B (incorrect) would involve significant manual effort and operational overhead, as all data encrypted with the old key would need to be re-encrypted with the new key, leading to potential downtime and complexity. Option C (incorrect) is not a supported feature for CMKs. While you can import key material, KMS does not provide an API to ''generate new key material and import it into existing CMKs'' for automatic rotation. Automatic rotation is a native feature. Option D (incorrect) would mean losing customer control over the key policy and auditability, as AWS-managed keys are managed by AWS, not by the customer, which likely violates the internal security policies requiring CMKs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_51',
  'A company stores highly confidential data in an Amazon S3 bucket. To meet stringent compliance requirements, all objects uploaded to this bucket must be encrypted using Server-Side Encryption with AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) with a specific Customer Managed Key (CMK). The security team needs to implement a preventive control that automatically rejects any `PutObject` requests that do not adhere to this encryption standard.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an S3 bucket policy that explicitly denies `s3:PutObject` requests if the `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption` header is not `aws:kms` or if the `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id` header does not match the specified CMK ARN.","Enable S3 Default Encryption for the bucket, specifying SSE-KMS with the required CMK as the default encryption.","Implement an AWS Config rule to detect and remediate S3 objects that are not encrypted with the specified SSE-KMS CMK.","Require all client applications to perform client-side encryption using the specified CMK before uploading objects to the S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a strong preventive control. An S3 bucket policy with conditions checking the `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption` and `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id` headers will explicitly deny any `PutObject` requests that do not meet the specified SSE-KMS and CMK requirements, ensuring only compliant objects are stored. Option B (incorrect) sets a default encryption for objects uploaded without specific encryption headers, but it does not prevent or reject uploads that explicitly specify a different or no encryption. Such objects would still be encrypted, but not necessarily with the desired CMK or even SSE-KMS if a different header is provided. Option C (incorrect) is a reactive control. AWS Config can detect non-compliant objects after they have been uploaded, but it does not prevent the initial upload of non-compliant data, which is the requirement for a preventive control. Option D (incorrect) shifts the responsibility of enforcement to the client applications. While client-side encryption is a valid approach, relying solely on it does not provide a strong, centralized, server-side enforcement mechanism at the S3 bucket level, and misconfigured clients could still upload non-compliant data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_52',
  'A financial institution needs to store sensitive customer transaction data in Amazon S3. Compliance regulations require that all encryption keys are customer-managed, auditable via AWS CloudTrail, and allow for granular access control. The solution must provide the LEAST operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK)","Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)","Server-Side Encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C)","Client-Side Encryption using a client-side master key stored in AWS CloudHSM"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK) meets the requirements. SSE-KMS with a CMK allows for customer-managed keys, provides detailed key usage logs in AWS CloudTrail, and enables granular access control through KMS key policies, all while being a managed service with low operational overhead. Option B (incorrect) Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) uses AWS-managed keys, which do not provide customer control over key policies or detailed audit trails for key usage, failing the compliance requirement. This represents the anti-pattern ap-5.1-001. Option C (incorrect) Server-Side Encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C) requires the customer to manage and provide encryption keys with each request, significantly increasing operational overhead compared to KMS. Option D (incorrect) Client-Side Encryption using a client-side master key stored in AWS CloudHSM would provide strong security but introduces significant operational overhead for key management and client-side encryption logic, which is not the LEAST operational overhead solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_53',
  'A financial institution stores sensitive customer data in Amazon S3 and requires detailed audit trails of key usage for compliance. Which Amazon S3 encryption option, combined with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS), should be implemented to meet this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3)","Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK)","Server-Side Encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C)","Client-Side Encryption with a client-side master key"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK). SSE-KMS with a CMK provides detailed key usage logs in AWS CloudTrail, custom key policies, and granular access control, which is essential for compliance requiring key audit trails. Option A (SSE-S3) uses AWS-managed keys, which do not provide customer-controlled key policies or CloudTrail audit logs for key usage, making it unsuitable for stringent compliance requirements. Option C (SSE-C) requires the customer to manage and provide encryption keys, and while it offers control, it doesn''t integrate with KMS for audit trails in the same way as SSE-KMS. Option D (Client-Side Encryption) involves encrypting data before sending it to S3, but the question specifically asks for an S3 encryption option combined with KMS for audit trails.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_54',
  'A company needs to manage cryptographic keys for a critical application with FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance. Which AWS service is specifically designed to provide hardware security modules (HSMs) that meet this standard?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS CloudHSM","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS CloudHSM. AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated Hardware Security Modules (HSMs) in the AWS Cloud that are FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated, making it suitable for applications with stringent compliance requirements for cryptographic key management. Option A (AWS Key Management Service) is a managed service for creating and controlling encryption keys, but its underlying HSMs are FIPS 140-2 Level 2 validated, and it does not offer dedicated, single-tenant HSMs that customers directly control. Option B (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing secrets like database credentials, not for FIPS 140-2 Level 3 key management. Option D (AWS Certificate Manager) is for provisioning and managing SSL/TLS certificates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_55',
  'To ensure data at rest encryption for a new Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database instance, a security architect must enable encryption. Which AWS service is used to manage the cryptographic keys for Amazon RDS encryption?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS CloudHSM"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS). Amazon RDS integrates with AWS KMS to encrypt database instances and snapshots at rest. When you enable encryption for an RDS instance, AWS KMS is used to manage the encryption keys. Option A (AWS Secrets Manager) is used for managing application secrets like database credentials, not for encrypting the database itself. Option B (AWS Certificate Manager) is for managing SSL/TLS certificates. Option D (AWS CloudHSM) can be used for custom key stores with KMS, but KMS itself is the direct service for managing RDS encryption keys.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_56',
  'A media company stores video archives in Amazon S3 and must ensure that these objects cannot be deleted or overwritten for a fixed period to comply with regulatory requirements. Which Amazon S3 feature should be used to enforce this immutability?',
  'mcq',
  '["S3 Versioning","S3 Lifecycle policies","S3 Object Lock","S3 Block Public Access"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is S3 Object Lock. S3 Object Lock provides Write Once Read Many (WORM) protection for objects, preventing them from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely. This feature is specifically designed to meet regulatory compliance requirements for data retention and immutability. Option A (S3 Versioning) keeps multiple versions of an object, but it does not prevent deletion of object versions or the bucket itself. Option B (S3 Lifecycle policies) automate object transitions and expirations, but they do not enforce immutability against deletion or overwriting. Option D (S3 Block Public Access) prevents public access to S3 buckets and objects, which is a different security control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_2_57',
  'Which AWS KMS feature allows you to define who can use a KMS key and how they can use it, directly attached to the key itself?',
  'mcq',
  '["IAM policies","Resource policies","Key policies","Grants"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Key policies. A key policy is the primary access control mechanism for an AWS KMS key. It defines who has permission to use the KMS key and what cryptographic operations they can perform. Option A (IAM policies) can grant permissions to use KMS keys, but they are attached to IAM identities (users, roles) rather than directly to the KMS key. Option B (Resource policies) is a general term for policies attached to resources; for KMS, this is specifically a key policy. Option D (Grants) provide temporary, fine-grained permissions to use KMS keys, but key policies are the foundational, long-term access control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_2',
  'medium'
);

