  'm_58_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_37',
  'Which AWS service simplifies the process of provisioning, managing, and deploying public and private SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services and connected resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) is the correct service. ACM handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing SSL/TLS certificates and makes them available for use with other AWS services like Elastic Load Balancing, Amazon CloudFront, and API Gateway. Option A (AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)) manages user permissions and access, not certificates. Option C (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) manages encryption keys, not SSL/TLS certificates. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) stores and rotates secrets like database credentials, not SSL/TLS certificates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_38',
  'A large enterprise needs to issue private SSL/TLS certificates for internal applications and services within its AWS environment. The security team requires full control over the certificate authority (CA) hierarchy. Which AWS service should be used to establish and manage this internal CA?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for public certificates","AWS Certificate Manager Private Certificate Authority (ACM Private CA)","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Certificate Manager Private Certificate Authority (ACM Private CA)) is the correct service. ACM Private CA allows customers to create and manage their own private certificate authorities, enabling them to issue private SSL/TLS certificates for internal use. This provides full control over the CA hierarchy. Option A (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) is for managing encryption keys, not for issuing certificates. Option B (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for public certificates) is used for provisioning and managing public SSL/TLS certificates, not for creating a private CA. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing secrets like database credentials, not for certificate authority management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_39',
  'A company needs to establish a secure, encrypted connection between its on-premises data center and its Amazon VPC. The connection must use standard IPsec VPN protocols. Which AWS service combination should be used for this hybrid connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) is the correct service for establishing a secure, encrypted connection between an on-premises data center and an Amazon VPC using standard IPsec VPN protocols over the public internet. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private network connection but does not inherently provide encryption unless combined with a VPN over the Direct Connect link. Option C (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network (CDN) and is not used for hybrid network connectivity. Option D (AWS Global Accelerator) improves application availability and performance but does not establish encrypted hybrid network connections.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_40',
  'To ensure data stored in Amazon S3 is always accessed over an encrypted channel, which method should be implemented to enforce HTTPS for all requests to an S3 bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Amazon S3 bucket policy that denies HTTP requests.","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket.","Use Amazon CloudFront with a custom SSL certificate.","Set the default encryption for the S3 bucket to SSE-S3."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Configure an Amazon S3 bucket policy that denies HTTP requests) is the correct method to enforce HTTPS for all requests to an S3 bucket. A bucket policy can be written to explicitly deny any requests that do not use ''aws:SecureTransport'': ''true''. Option B (Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket) speeds up transfers but does not enforce HTTPS. Option C (Use Amazon CloudFront with a custom SSL certificate) can enforce HTTPS between clients and CloudFront, but it doesn''t directly enforce HTTPS for direct S3 access. Option D (Set the default encryption for the S3 bucket to SSE-S3) encrypts data at rest but does not enforce encryption for data in transit (HTTPS).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_41',
  'A company hosts a public website using Amazon S3 for static content and Amazon CloudFront for content delivery. The security team requires that all traffic between clients and CloudFront, as well as between CloudFront and S3, must be encrypted using HTTPS. Which configuration is necessary for CloudFront to meet this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the CloudFront distribution to use HTTP only for viewers and origin.","Ensure the CloudFront distribution is configured for ''HTTPS Only'' for viewer protocol policy and ''HTTPS Only'' or ''Match Viewer'' for origin protocol policy.","Attach an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key to the CloudFront distribution.","Enable Amazon S3 Block Public Access settings for the S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Ensure the CloudFront distribution is configured for ''HTTPS Only'' for viewer protocol policy and ''HTTPS Only'' or ''Match Viewer'' for origin protocol policy) is the correct configuration. The ''Viewer Protocol Policy'' setting on a CloudFront distribution controls the protocol between clients and CloudFront, and ''HTTPS Only'' ensures encryption. The ''Origin Protocol Policy'' controls the protocol between CloudFront and the origin (S3 in this case), and ''HTTPS Only'' or ''Match Viewer'' ensures encryption for that segment. Option A (Configure the CloudFront distribution to use HTTP only for viewers and origin) would explicitly disable encryption, which is the opposite of the requirement. Option C (Attach an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key to the CloudFront distribution) is incorrect; KMS is for data at rest encryption, not for managing TLS for data in transit with CloudFront. Option D (Enable Amazon S3 Block Public Access settings for the S3 bucket) is a security measure for S3 public access but does not directly configure HTTPS enforcement for CloudFront.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_42',
  'Which AWS service provides a dedicated, private network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS, and can be combined with IPsec VPN for an encrypted hybrid connection?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Transit Gateway"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated, private network connection between an on-premises data center and AWS. While Direct Connect itself does not encrypt traffic, it can be combined with an AWS Site-to-Site VPN over the Direct Connect connection to provide an encrypted hybrid connection. Option A (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) provides an encrypted connection over the public internet. Option B (Amazon VPC Peering) connects two VPCs within AWS, not an on-premises data center. Option D (AWS Transit Gateway) connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks but relies on VPN or Direct Connect for the physical connectivity to on-premises.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_43',
  'A development team is building a RESTful API using Amazon API Gateway. The security team requires that all communication with the API Gateway endpoints must be encrypted using HTTPS. How can this be enforced for API Gateway?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a resource policy on the API Gateway to allow only HTTP traffic.","Enable the ''Require HTTPS'' setting on the API Gateway custom domain name or use the default endpoint which enforces HTTPS.","Attach an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key to the API Gateway.","Use an Amazon S3 bucket policy to restrict access to the API Gateway."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Enable the ''Require HTTPS'' setting on the API Gateway custom domain name or use the default endpoint which enforces HTTPS) is the correct way to enforce HTTPS for API Gateway. By default, API Gateway''s execute-api endpoints only support HTTPS. If a custom domain name is used, you can configure it to ''Require HTTPS'' to ensure all traffic is encrypted. Option A (Configure a resource policy on the API Gateway to allow only HTTP traffic) is incorrect and would violate the requirement. Option C (Attach an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key to the API Gateway) is incorrect; KMS is for data at rest encryption, not for enforcing HTTPS for API Gateway. Option D (Use an Amazon S3 bucket policy to restrict access to the API Gateway) is incorrect; S3 bucket policies control access to S3, not API Gateway.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_44',
  'A security engineer needs to import an existing SSL/TLS certificate issued by a third-party Certificate Authority (CA) into AWS for use with an Application Load Balancer. Which AWS service is designed to manage and deploy such certificates?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) is the correct service. ACM allows you to import SSL/TLS certificates issued by third-party CAs, as well as provision new public certificates directly. These certificates can then be easily deployed to integrated AWS services like Application Load Balancers. Option B (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) is for managing encryption keys, not SSL/TLS certificates. Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is for storing and rotating secrets like database credentials, not certificates. Option D (AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)) manages user permissions and access, not certificates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_45',
  'A public-facing web application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB) handles sensitive customer data. The company''s security policy mandates that all traffic to the application must be encrypted using HTTPS. Currently, the ALB is configured with both HTTP (port 80) and HTTPS (port 443) listeners. The security team needs to enforce HTTPS for all incoming traffic with minimal operational overhead and without breaking existing bookmarks or links that might use HTTP.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a listener rule on the ALB for the HTTP (port 80) listener to redirect all incoming requests to the HTTPS (port 443) listener using a 301 (permanent) redirect.","Remove the HTTP (port 80) listener from the Application Load Balancer.","Implement AWS WAF to block all HTTP requests arriving at the Application Load Balancer.","Modify the application code on the EC2 instances to perform an HTTP to HTTPS redirect."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective and efficient solution. Configuring a 301 permanent redirect on the ALB''s HTTP listener ensures that all unencrypted traffic is automatically and transparently redirected to the secure HTTPS endpoint. This enforces encryption, preserves existing HTTP links, and minimizes operational overhead as it''s managed at the load balancer level. This directly addresses anti-pattern ap-5.2-001. Option B (incorrect) would immediately break any existing HTTP links or bookmarks, leading to a poor user experience and potential loss of access for users who don''t manually switch to HTTPS. Option C (incorrect) would block HTTP traffic but would not redirect it, resulting in users seeing an error page instead of being seamlessly moved to the secure site. AWS WAF is primarily for filtering malicious traffic, not for redirection. Option D (incorrect) would work, but it shifts the responsibility for redirection to the application layer, increasing the application''s complexity, consuming EC2 instance resources, and requiring code changes and deployments, which is less efficient than handling it at the ALB level.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_46',
  'A company hosts a public-facing web application that requires HTTPS to secure communication between clients and its Application Load Balancer (ALB). The security team needs a scalable and managed solution to provision, deploy, and renew public TLS certificates for the ALB, ensuring high availability and minimal manual intervention.',
  'mcq',
  '["Request a public certificate from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) and associate it with the HTTPS listener on the Application Load Balancer.","Purchase a public TLS certificate from a third-party Certificate Authority (CA) and manually install it on each Amazon EC2 instance behind the ALB.","Generate a self-signed TLS certificate and upload it to the Application Load Balancer.","Deploy AWS Certificate Manager Private CA to issue certificates for the public-facing application."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the recommended and most efficient solution. AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) simplifies the provisioning, management, and deployment of public SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services like Application Load Balancers. ACM automatically handles certificate renewal, eliminating manual intervention and ensuring continuous security. Option B (incorrect) would involve significant manual operational overhead for installation on each EC2 instance and for periodic renewal, which is not scalable or managed. ALBs are designed to terminate TLS, so certificates should be on the ALB, not the instances. Option C (incorrect) would result in untrusted connections for public users, as self-signed certificates are not recognized by web browsers and would trigger security warnings. Option D (incorrect) is designed for issuing private certificates for internal use cases, such as internal applications or microservices, not for public-facing web applications that require certificates trusted by public web browsers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_47',
  'A large enterprise operates a complex microservices architecture with numerous internal services communicating with each other. To enhance security and establish mutual trust, the security team requires all internal service-to-service communication to use mTLS (mutual TLS). They need a scalable, managed solution to issue and manage private certificates for these internal services, ensuring that the certificates are trusted only within their private network.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy AWS Certificate Manager Private CA to create and manage a private certificate authority hierarchy for issuing internal certificates.","Request public certificates from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for each internal service.","Set up and manage an on-premises Certificate Authority (CA) to issue internal certificates.","Generate self-signed certificates for each microservice and distribute them manually."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the ideal solution for this scenario. AWS Certificate Manager Private CA allows enterprises to create a private CA hierarchy, which can then be used to issue private certificates for internal resources, such as microservices. These certificates are trusted within the organization''s private network, enabling mTLS for secure internal communication without the need for public trust. Option B (incorrect) is not suitable because public ACM certificates are issued by public CAs and are trusted by public browsers. Using them for internal services is unnecessary, could lead to misconfigurations, and doesn''t align with the requirement for private trust. Option C (incorrect) would involve significant operational overhead, including hardware, software, security, and maintenance of the CA infrastructure, which contradicts the benefits of a managed cloud service. Option D (incorrect) is highly insecure and unmanageable for a large microservices architecture. Self-signed certificates lack a central trust anchor, making certificate distribution, revocation, and overall trust management extremely complex and prone to errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_48',
  'A company hosts a static website on an Amazon S3 bucket, which is configured for public access. The security team has identified that some users are still accessing the website via HTTP, compromising data in transit. They need to implement a preventive control at the S3 bucket level to ensure that all access to the website content is strictly enforced over HTTPS, rejecting any unencrypted requests.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an S3 bucket policy that explicitly denies `s3:GetObject` requests if the `aws:SecureTransport` condition is false.","Place an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the S3 bucket and configure it to redirect HTTP to HTTPS.","Implement an AWS WAF Web ACL to block all HTTP requests targeting the S3 bucket.","Modify the static website content to include JavaScript that redirects HTTP to HTTPS."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most direct and effective preventive control at the S3 bucket level. An S3 bucket policy using the `aws:SecureTransport` condition ensures that any request made over HTTP (where `aws:SecureTransport` is false) to retrieve objects (`s3:GetObject`) is explicitly denied, thereby enforcing HTTPS for all access. Option B (incorrect) is a valid architectural pattern for serving static websites over HTTPS and can redirect HTTP to HTTPS. However, the question asks for a preventive control at the S3 bucket level to *reject* unencrypted requests, implying direct S3 access. CloudFront would sit in front of S3, and direct S3 access might still be possible if not explicitly blocked by the bucket policy. Option C (incorrect) cannot directly block HTTP requests to an S3 bucket in this manner. AWS WAF integrates with services like CloudFront, Application Load Balancer, and API Gateway, but not directly with S3 for this type of enforcement. Option D (incorrect) is a client-side solution and is not a reliable or robust server-side enforcement. It relies on the client''s browser executing JavaScript and does not prevent the initial unencrypted HTTP request from reaching the S3 bucket.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_49',
  'An organization needs to establish a secure and encrypted connection between its on-premises data center and its Amazon VPC to access private resources. The solution must ensure that all data transmitted over this hybrid connection is protected from eavesdropping and tampering, adhering to corporate security standards for data in transit.',
  'mcq',
  '["Establish an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection between the on-premises network and the Amazon VPC.","Use AWS Direct Connect to create a dedicated network connection between the on-premises data center and AWS.","Configure a public Internet Gateway for the VPC and route traffic over the public internet.","Set up a Bastion Host in the Amazon VPC and tunnel all traffic through it."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the standard and most appropriate solution for encrypted hybrid connectivity. An AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection creates a secure IPsec tunnel over the public internet, ensuring that all data transmitted between the on-premises data center and the Amazon VPC is encrypted and protected. Option B (incorrect) provides a dedicated, private network connection, but it does not encrypt data in transit by default. While Direct Connect can be combined with a VPN for encryption, Direct Connect alone does not fulfill the encryption requirement. Option C (incorrect) would route traffic over the public internet without encryption, making it highly vulnerable to eavesdropping and tampering, which violates the security standards. Option D (incorrect) is typically used for secure administrative access to private instances within a VPC, not for establishing a scalable, encrypted site-to-site network connection for general traffic between an on-premises data center and a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_50',
  'A large enterprise requires a dedicated, high-bandwidth, and low-latency network connection between its on-premises data center and AWS. Furthermore, all data traversing this connection must be encrypted end-to-end to meet stringent regulatory compliance requirements. The security architect needs to design a hybrid connectivity solution that satisfies all these criteria.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement AWS Direct Connect for dedicated connectivity and establish an IPsec VPN over the Direct Connect connection for encryption.","Utilize only AWS Direct Connect to provide the dedicated, high-bandwidth, low-latency connection.","Set up an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection over the public internet to encrypt traffic.","Configure AWS PrivateLink to provide private connectivity to AWS services from the on-premises network."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the comprehensive solution that meets all requirements. AWS Direct Connect provides the dedicated, high-bandwidth, low-latency connection. To add end-to-end encryption, an IPsec VPN is established over the Direct Connect connection, ensuring that all data in transit is encrypted for compliance. Option B (incorrect) provides dedicated, high-bandwidth, low-latency connectivity, but it does not inherently encrypt data in transit. Data over Direct Connect is private but not encrypted unless a VPN is layered on top. Option C (incorrect) provides encryption over the public internet, but it does not offer the dedicated bandwidth or guaranteed low-latency characteristics of Direct Connect, failing to meet those specific requirements. Option D (incorrect) provides private connectivity to specific AWS services (like S3 or EC2 APIs) from a VPC or on-premises network, but it is not a solution for general site-to-site network encryption or dedicated high-bandwidth connectivity between an entire on-premises data center and a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_51',
  'A company has deployed a REST API using Amazon API Gateway to expose sensitive customer data. The security team mandates that all client-to-API communication must be secured using HTTPS to protect data in transit. The solution should be managed by AWS and require minimal custom code.',
  'mcq',
  '["Ensure the API Gateway custom domain is configured with an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate and that the API Gateway endpoint type inherently supports HTTPS.","Implement HTTPS redirection logic within the AWS Lambda function integrated with API Gateway.","Configure an Amazon S3 bucket policy to enforce HTTPS for API Gateway requests.","Deploy an AWS WAF Web ACL to block all HTTP requests directed to the API Gateway."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the native and most efficient way to enforce HTTPS for API Gateway. API Gateway endpoints (EDGE or REGIONAL) inherently support HTTPS. When using a custom domain, an ACM certificate must be associated with it, and API Gateway handles the TLS termination, ensuring all client-to-API communication is encrypted. Option B (incorrect) is unnecessary and inefficient. API Gateway handles TLS termination before requests reach integrated Lambda functions, so implementing HTTPS redirection within Lambda would add complexity and latency without providing additional security for client-to-API transit. Option C (incorrect) is irrelevant. Amazon S3 bucket policies are for S3 buckets, not for enforcing HTTPS on API Gateway endpoints. Option D (incorrect) would block HTTP requests, but it would not redirect them to HTTPS. Users would simply receive an error instead of being seamlessly directed to the secure endpoint. API Gateway''s native HTTPS support with ACM is the correct approach for enforcement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_52',
  'A web application uses an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to distribute traffic to Amazon EC2 instances. The security team mandates that all traffic must be encrypted using HTTPS, even if clients attempt to connect over HTTP. What is the MOST efficient way to enforce this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the ALB to have only an HTTPS listener and remove the HTTP listener.","Use an AWS WAF rule to block all HTTP requests before they reach the ALB.","Implement an ALB listener rule to redirect HTTP (port 80) traffic to HTTPS (port 443).","Modify the application code on the EC2 instances to redirect HTTP requests to HTTPS."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to implement an ALB listener rule to redirect HTTP (port 80) traffic to HTTPS (port 443). This is the most efficient and recommended way to enforce HTTPS at the load balancer level. It ensures that any client attempting to connect via HTTP is automatically redirected to the secure HTTPS endpoint, preventing unencrypted transmission. Option A (configuring only an HTTPS listener) would block all HTTP traffic, but it would not redirect clients, leading to connection errors for users who try to access the HTTP endpoint. Option B (using AWS WAF) is possible but less efficient for a simple HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect, as WAF is typically used for more advanced web attack protection. Option D (modifying application code) would work but shifts the responsibility to the application layer, increasing operational overhead and making it less centralized than an ALB rule.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_53',
  'A large enterprise requires issuing private TLS certificates for internal applications and services within their AWS environment, managed by their own Certificate Authority (CA). Which AWS service should be used for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Certificate Manager Private CA","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Certificate Manager Private CA. This service allows you to create and manage your own private Certificate Authority (CA) hierarchy, enabling you to issue private certificates for internal use. Option A (AWS Certificate Manager) manages public SSL/TLS certificates provided by Amazon or imported third-party certificates, but it does not allow you to create your own private CA. Option C (AWS Key Management Service) is for managing encryption keys, not for issuing certificates. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing application secrets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_54',
  'A company needs to establish a secure, encrypted connection between its on-premises data center and its Amazon VPC over the public internet. Which AWS service provides a managed solution for this hybrid connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Site-to-Site VPN. This service provides a secure, encrypted connection between your on-premises network and your Amazon VPC over the public internet. Option A (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private network connection from your premises to AWS, which is not over the public internet. Option C (Amazon VPC Peering) connects two VPCs within AWS, not an on-premises data center. Option D (AWS Transit Gateway) connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, but it relies on VPN or Direct Connect for the actual encrypted hybrid connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_55',
  'Which AWS service simplifies the process of provisioning, managing, and deploying public and private SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services like Elastic Load Balancing and Amazon CloudFront?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS CloudHSM"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). ACM handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing SSL/TLS certificates and makes them available for use with other AWS services such as Elastic Load Balancing, Amazon CloudFront, and API Gateway. Option A (AWS Key Management Service) is for managing cryptographic keys, not SSL/TLS certificates. Option B (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing application secrets. Option D (AWS CloudHSM) provides dedicated hardware security modules for cryptographic operations and key storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_58_m_58_1_56',
  'A security team requires that all access to an Amazon S3 bucket containing sensitive data must use HTTPS. How can this be enforced directly on the S3 bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an S3 bucket policy that denies HTTP requests.","Enable S3 Block Public Access settings for the bucket.","Use an AWS WAF rule to filter requests to the S3 bucket.","Place the S3 bucket behind an Amazon CloudFront distribution and enforce HTTPS on CloudFront."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is to configure an S3 bucket policy that denies HTTP requests. A bucket policy can be written to explicitly deny any request that does not use HTTPS, ensuring all data in transit to and from the bucket is encrypted. Option B (S3 Block Public Access) prevents public access but does not specifically enforce HTTPS for allowed access. Option C (AWS WAF) cannot directly protect an S3 bucket unless it''s fronted by an Amazon CloudFront distribution or an Application Load Balancer, which is not direct enforcement on the bucket itself. Option D (CloudFront distribution) is a valid approach for web content delivery, but the question asks for enforcement directly on the S3 bucket.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain58_12',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_58_1',
  'medium'
);

