INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_81',
  'Which AWS services are primarily used to establish a secure, multi-account AWS environment and enforce governance across them? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Control Tower","AWS Organizations","AWS CloudFormation for infrastructure as code deployments","Amazon S3 for object storage","Amazon EC2 for virtual servers"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Control Tower) provides a simplified way to set up and govern a new, secure, multi-account AWS environment. Option B (AWS Organizations) helps you centrally manage and govern your environment as you grow and scale your AWS resources. AWS CloudFormation (Option C) is for provisioning infrastructure, but not primarily for multi-account governance. Amazon S3 (Option D) is object storage, and Amazon EC2 (Option E) provides compute capacity, neither of which are core services for multi-account governance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_82',
  'Which features are provided by AWS Organizations to manage multiple AWS accounts centrally? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Lambda functions for serverless compute","Amazon DynamoDB tables for application data","Service Control Policies (SCPs)","Organizational Units (OUs)","Amazon S3 bucket policies for data access"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (Service Control Policies or SCPs) allow you to centrally manage permissions in your AWS Organization, acting as guardrails. Option D (Organizational Units or OUs) enable you to group accounts into a hierarchical structure for easier management and policy application. AWS Lambda functions (Option A), Amazon DynamoDB tables (Option B), and Amazon S3 bucket policies (Option E) are individual service features or compute services, not core features of AWS Organizations for central account management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_83',
  'To ensure consistent security configurations and compliance across a multi-account environment, which services can be used to define and deploy standardized resource configurations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SQS for messaging","AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Lambda for compute","AWS Control Tower","Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (AWS Config conformance packs) allow you to create a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that can be deployed as a single entity across an Organization to check for compliance. Option D (AWS Control Tower) helps set up a landing zone and applies mandatory and strongly recommended guardrails, which include AWS Config rules, to ensure ongoing governance. Amazon SQS (Option A), AWS Lambda (Option C), and Amazon CloudWatch (Option E) are not primarily used for defining and deploying standardized resource configurations for multi-account compliance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_84',
  'An organization needs to automate the creation of new AWS accounts with pre-configured security baselines and ongoing governance. Which services are best suited for this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets for multi-account deployment","AWS Service Catalog for approved product provisioning","Amazon EC2 instances for compute","AWS Organizations","AWS Control Tower"]'::json,
  '{3,4}',
  'Option D (AWS Organizations) allows you to programmatically create new AWS accounts, group them into Organizational Units, and apply policies. Option E (AWS Control Tower) builds on AWS Organizations to automate the setup of a secure, multi-account AWS environment (a landing zone) and provides ongoing governance and guardrails. AWS CloudFormation StackSets (Option A) can deploy resources across accounts but doesn''t manage account creation and governance at the landing zone level. AWS Service Catalog (Option B) provides self-service access to approved products, not account vending. Amazon EC2 instances (Option C) are compute resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_85',
  'Which of the following are key benefits of adopting a multi-account strategy using AWS Organizations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Increased operational overhead for security teams","Reduced security isolation between environments","Blast radius containment","Simplified billing management","Centralized application deployment for all workloads"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (Blast radius containment) is a key benefit, as security incidents in one account are less likely to affect others. Option D (Simplified billing management) is also a benefit, as AWS Organizations allows for consolidated billing across all accounts. Option A (Increased operational overhead) is incorrect; while there''s initial setup, it generally reduces long-term operational overhead for security and governance. Option B (Reduced security isolation) is the opposite of a benefit. Option E (Centralized application deployment for all workloads) is an anti-pattern (ap-6.1-001) as it negates the benefits of isolation provided by a multi-account strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_86',
  'Which AWS services allow for the centralized management of security standards and the detection of deviations from those standards across multiple accounts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation for infrastructure as code","AWS Config","AWS Service Catalog for approved resource provisioning","Amazon S3 for object storage","Amazon Security Hub"]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (AWS Config) enables you to assess, audit, and evaluate the configurations of your AWS resources, including cross-account aggregation of compliance data. Option E (Amazon Security Hub) provides a comprehensive view of your high-priority security alerts and compliance status across multiple AWS accounts and Regions. AWS CloudFormation (Option A) is for provisioning resources. AWS Service Catalog (Option C) is for self-service provisioning of approved products. Amazon S3 (Option D) is for object storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_87',
  'A large enterprise is establishing a new multi-account AWS environment and needs to centrally manage governance and security guardrails across all accounts. They want to ensure consistent account provisioning and prevent unauthorized actions. Which AWS services are fundamental for achieving this centralized management and preventive control? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Control Tower for setting up a landing zone and guardrails.","AWS Organizations for creating an OU hierarchy and applying SCPs.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings from member accounts.","AWS Config for recording resource configuration changes.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning pre-approved resources."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a simplified way to set up and govern a secure, multi-account AWS environment, including automated account provisioning and preventive/detective guardrails. Option B (correct) is the foundational service for centrally managing multiple AWS accounts, allowing for the creation of Organizational Units (OUs) and the application of Service Control Policies (SCPs) to enforce permissions across accounts. Option C (incorrect) is primarily for aggregating and managing security findings, which is a detective control, not a direct mechanism for centralized governance or preventive control deployment. Option D (incorrect) records configuration changes and evaluates compliance, which is a detective control, not a primary tool for centralized governance or preventive guardrails. Option E (incorrect) is for enabling users to quickly deploy pre-approved AWS resources, which supports consistent deployment but is not the core service for centralized account governance or preventive guardrails at the organizational level.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_88',
  'An organization is designing its AWS Organizations structure to improve security posture and simplify compliance. They need to ensure that specific security policies are enforced across different environments (e.g., Development, Staging, Production) and that new accounts adhere to these policies. Which two capabilities of AWS Organizations directly support these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Creating Organizational Units (OUs) to group accounts with similar security requirements.","Implementing Service Control Policies (SCPs) to restrict permissions for accounts within OUs.","Integrating with AWS Control Tower for automated landing zone setup.","Centralizing billing for all member accounts.","Delegating administrative access for specific services to member accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for logical grouping of accounts based on their function, environment, or security posture, which is crucial for applying targeted policies. Option B (correct) provides a powerful way to set maximum permissions for accounts within an OU, enforcing security guardrails and preventing unauthorized actions, thus ensuring new accounts adhere to policies. Option C (incorrect) describes a service that builds upon AWS Organizations but is not a direct capability of Organizations itself for policy enforcement. Option D (incorrect) is a feature of AWS Organizations but is related to cost management, not directly to enforcing security policies or account adherence. Option E (incorrect) is a feature for operational delegation, not primarily for enforcing security policies across OUs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_89',
  'A security architect is tasked with establishing a new multi-account environment that includes a well-architected landing zone and continuous governance. The solution must provide automated account provisioning and implement preventive guardrails out-of-the-box. Which two services are essential for meeting these requirements efficiently? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Control Tower for automated landing zone setup and guardrails.","AWS Organizations for managing the account structure and applying SCPs.","Amazon Security Hub for centralizing security findings.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying resources across accounts.","AWS Service Catalog for offering self-service provisioning of approved products."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a managed service that sets up a secure, multi-account AWS environment (landing zone), automates account provisioning, and implements preventive and detective guardrails. Option B (correct) is the underlying service that AWS Control Tower uses to manage the organizational structure, create Organizational Units (OUs), and apply Service Control Policies (SCPs) for preventive controls. Option C (incorrect) is for aggregating security findings, which is a detective control, not for setting up the landing zone or implementing preventive guardrails directly. Option D (incorrect) is for deploying infrastructure as code across multiple accounts but doesn''t provide the full landing zone setup or automated guardrails of Control Tower. Option E (incorrect) is for self-service provisioning of approved resources, not for establishing the foundational multi-account governance and guardrails.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_90',
  'An organization wants to improve its security posture by centralizing the aggregation of security findings and compliance status across all accounts and Regions within its AWS Organization. This will provide a unified view for security operations. Which AWS services can be configured to achieve this centralized aggregation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Security Hub for cross-account and cross-Region aggregation.","AWS Config for multi-account, multi-Region data aggregation.","AWS Control Tower for managing guardrails and account provisioning.","AWS Organizations for defining the account structure.","AWS Audit Manager for collecting compliance evidence."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for the aggregation of security findings from various AWS services (like GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie) and partner solutions into a single pane of glass, supporting cross-account and cross-Region aggregation. Option B (correct) enables the aggregation of configuration data and compliance status from AWS Config recorders across multiple accounts and Regions into a central account. Option C (incorrect) is primarily for setting up and governing a multi-account environment with guardrails, not for aggregating security findings or compliance status. Option D (incorrect) is for managing the organizational structure and applying policies, not for aggregating security findings or configuration data. Option E (incorrect) is for automating the collection of evidence for audit purposes, not for real-time aggregation of security findings or configuration compliance status.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_91',
  'A company is expanding its AWS footprint and needs to ensure that all new accounts provisioned for different departments adhere to a baseline set of security and compliance standards. They also require a mechanism to continuously monitor these accounts for non-compliance. Which AWS services are best suited to establish this baseline and monitor compliance across a multi-account environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs for deploying standardized compliance rules.","AWS Organizations with Service Control Policies (SCPs) for enforcing preventive controls.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings and compliance checks.","AWS Service Catalog for self-service provisioning of approved resources.","AWS Audit Manager for automated evidence collection for audits."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for the deployment of a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single entity across multiple accounts, ensuring a standardized baseline for compliance. Option B (incorrect) provides preventive controls by setting maximum permissions, which is crucial for security, but AWS Config conformance packs and Security Hub are more directly involved in monitoring and reporting compliance status. Option C (correct) aggregates security findings and compliance checks from various AWS services and industry standards, providing a centralized view of compliance posture across accounts. Option D (incorrect) helps in provisioning pre-approved resources but doesn''t directly establish or monitor compliance baselines. Option E (incorrect) automates evidence collection for audits but is not the primary service for establishing and continuously monitoring compliance baselines.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_92',
  'A security team is implementing a multi-account strategy and wants to ensure that no account can launch resources in unsupported Regions or disable critical logging services. They also need a way to easily provision new accounts that conform to these baseline restrictions. Which AWS services provide the capabilities to enforce these preventive controls and streamline account creation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Organizations with Service Control Policies (SCPs).","AWS Control Tower for automated account provisioning and guardrails.","AWS Config rules for detecting non-compliant resources.","Amazon Security Hub for centralizing security findings.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows administrators to create Service Control Policies (SCPs) that define the maximum available permissions for accounts within an Organizational Unit (OU), effectively preventing actions like launching resources in specific Regions or disabling logging. Option B (correct) automates the setup of a secure, multi-account AWS environment (landing zone) and includes automated account provisioning through Account Factory, which ensures new accounts are created with predefined guardrails and best practices. Option C (incorrect) is a detective control that identifies non-compliant resources after they are created, rather than preventing the action. Option D (incorrect) aggregates security findings, which is a detective control, not a preventive enforcement mechanism. Option E (incorrect) is for deploying infrastructure as code across multiple accounts but doesn''t inherently provide the preventive policy enforcement or automated account provisioning capabilities needed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_93',
  'An organization is implementing a robust multi-account strategy to enhance security and operational efficiency. They want to ensure that each environment (e.g., development, production) is isolated, and that security policies are consistently applied and enforced across these environments. Which two core features of AWS Organizations facilitate this isolation and policy enforcement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Organizational Units (OUs) for logical grouping and isolation of accounts.","Service Control Policies (SCPs) for defining maximum permissions and enforcing guardrails.","Consolidated billing for simplified financial management.","AWS Control Tower for automated landing zone setup.","Delegated administration for specific services."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for the logical grouping of AWS accounts into Organizational Units (OUs), which provides a clear hierarchy for applying policies and effectively isolates different environments. Option B (correct) are powerful policies that you can use to manage permissions in your organization, acting as guardrails to set the maximum available permissions for accounts within an OU, thus enforcing consistent security policies. Option C (incorrect) is a feature of AWS Organizations but is related to cost management, not directly to security isolation or policy enforcement. Option D (incorrect) is a service that builds on AWS Organizations to automate the setup of a landing zone, but OUs and SCPs are the direct features within Organizations for isolation and policy enforcement. Option E (incorrect) is a feature for delegating administrative tasks for specific services, not for general security isolation or policy enforcement across OUs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_94',
  'A global enterprise with over 100 AWS accounts, organized into several Organizational Units (OUs) within AWS Organizations, is establishing a new cloud governance framework. The security team''s primary objective is to enforce a consistent security baseline across all accounts, prevent the provisioning of non-compliant resources, and maintain a centralized view of security posture. The solution must minimize operational overhead for the security team and provide a self-service mechanism for development teams to provision secure environments. Which two AWS services are fundamental for achieving these goals effectively? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Control Tower to establish a multi-account landing zone with preventive and detective guardrails.","AWS Service Catalog to enable self-service provisioning of pre-approved and compliant AWS resources.","Amazon GuardDuty to continuously monitor for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior across all accounts.","AWS Config to record configuration changes and evaluate resources against desired compliance rules.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy infrastructure as code templates across multiple accounts and Regions."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is fundamental because AWS Control Tower establishes a well-architected multi-account AWS environment, known as a landing zone, with built-in preventive and detective guardrails managed through AWS Organizations SCPs and AWS Config rules. This ensures a consistent security baseline from the outset. Option B (correct) is crucial for enabling self-service provisioning of pre-approved and compliant resources. AWS Service Catalog allows administrators to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, ensuring that developers can only provision resources that adhere to the organization''s security and compliance standards. Option C (incorrect) is a detective control that monitors for threats but does not prevent the provisioning of non-compliant resources or provide a self-service mechanism. While important for security, it doesn''t directly address the core requirements of consistent baseline enforcement and compliant self-service provisioning. Option D (incorrect) is a detective control that evaluates resource configurations against rules. While essential for continuous compliance monitoring, AWS Config alone does not provide the preventive guardrails or the self-service provisioning mechanism required by the scenario. Option E (incorrect) is a powerful tool for deploying infrastructure as code, but it requires significant manual effort to manage templates, permissions, and ensure compliance without the higher-level governance and self-service capabilities offered by Control Tower and Service Catalog.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_95',
  'A large financial institution is migrating its critical workloads to AWS and requires a robust multi-account strategy to ensure strict isolation between production, development, and testing environments, as well as segregation of duties for administrative access. The security team must implement controls that prevent any account from deploying resources that violate regulatory compliance standards, even if an administrator in that account attempts to override policies. Furthermore, the solution needs to aggregate security findings centrally for comprehensive oversight. Which two AWS Organizations features, combined with a central security service, are most effective in meeting these stringent requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing Service Control Policies (SCPs) at the Organizational Unit (OU) level to define maximum permissions for accounts.","Designing a hierarchical OU structure to group accounts by environment and sensitivity, enabling targeted policy application.","Utilizing AWS Control Tower''s Account Factory to provision new accounts with a predefined baseline.","Configuring AWS Config conformance packs for continuous auditing against compliance standards across all accounts.","Delegating administration of Amazon Security Hub to a central security account for cross-account aggregation of findings."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is essential because Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations define the maximum available permissions for all accounts in an OU or the entire organization. This provides preventive guardrails, ensuring that no account, regardless of its IAM permissions, can perform actions that violate the SCP, thus enforcing regulatory compliance at a foundational level. Option B (correct) is crucial for effective policy management. A well-designed hierarchical OU structure allows the institution to group accounts based on their function (e.g., production, development) and sensitivity, enabling the application of specific SCPs and other policies to relevant groups of accounts, thereby enforcing strict isolation and segregation of duties. Option C (incorrect) is a valuable feature of AWS Control Tower for consistent account provisioning, but the question specifically asks for AWS Organizations features combined with a central security service. While Control Tower uses Organizations, the Account Factory itself is a Control Tower feature, not solely an Organizations feature. Option D (incorrect) is a detective control that audits compliance post-deployment. While important, it doesn''t *prevent* non-compliant resource deployment, which is a key requirement of the scenario (preventing violations even if an administrator attempts to override). Option E (incorrect) describes a correct practice for centralizing security findings with Amazon Security Hub, which is a central security service. However, the question asks for *two AWS Organizations features* combined with a central security service. While Security Hub aggregation is important, it''s not an Organizations feature itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_96',
  'A rapidly growing technology company is expanding its AWS footprint across multiple regions and needs to ensure that all new AWS accounts are provisioned with a standardized security baseline, including specific IAM roles, network configurations, and logging settings. The security team wants to automate the account creation process and apply mandatory preventive controls to restrict actions like disabling AWS CloudTrail or deleting Amazon S3 buckets containing audit logs. The solution must also allow for centralized management of these guardrails and provide a streamlined, self-service experience for internal teams requesting new accounts. Which two capabilities, when combined, best address these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using AWS Control Tower''s Account Factory to automate the provisioning of new accounts with a pre-configured baseline.","Implementing Service Control Policies (SCPs) within AWS Organizations to enforce preventive guardrails across all accounts.","Deploying AWS CloudFormation StackSets to distribute security configurations to newly provisioned accounts.","Leveraging AWS Service Catalog to offer pre-approved security configurations as products for self-service deployment.","Configuring Amazon Security Hub to aggregate security findings from all accounts for centralized monitoring."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is essential because AWS Control Tower''s Account Factory automates the provisioning of new AWS accounts, ensuring they are created with a predefined baseline that includes necessary IAM roles, network configurations, and logging. This standardizes the account creation process and embeds initial security settings. Option B (correct) is crucial for enforcing mandatory preventive controls. Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations allow the security team to define maximum permissions for accounts, preventing actions like disabling CloudTrail or deleting audit logs, even if an account administrator attempts to do so. This provides strong, centralized guardrails. Option C (incorrect) is a valid deployment mechanism but is typically used for deploying resources *within* existing accounts, not for the initial provisioning of the account itself with a full baseline and preventive guardrails in the same integrated manner as Control Tower. Option D (incorrect) is excellent for self-service provisioning of *resources* within an account, but the primary requirement here is for provisioning *accounts* with a baseline and applying preventive controls at the organizational level, which is better handled by Control Tower and SCPs. Option E (incorrect) is a detective control for centralized monitoring of security findings, which is important, but it does not address the core requirements of automated account provisioning with a baseline or the enforcement of preventive controls.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_97',
  'A large enterprise has adopted a multi-account strategy using AWS Organizations, with separate accounts for different environments (e.g., development, staging, production) and business units. The central security team is responsible for monitoring the overall security posture and ensuring compliance across the entire organization. They need to aggregate security findings from various AWS services (like Amazon GuardDuty, AWS Config, and AWS IAM Access Analyzer) from all member accounts into a single, centralized view. Furthermore, they require the ability to manage and respond to these findings from this central security account without needing to log into individual member accounts. Which two capabilities are essential for the security team to implement this centralized security operations model efficiently? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enabling cross-account aggregation in Amazon Security Hub and designating a delegated administrator account.","Deploying AWS Config conformance packs to all accounts to enforce a consistent set of security best practices.","Implementing AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) to restrict unauthorized actions across the organization.","Configuring AWS Audit Manager to collect evidence for compliance audits across all member accounts.","Setting up cross-account roles in each member account to allow the central security account to assume roles for monitoring and management."]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (correct) is critical for centralizing security findings. Amazon Security Hub allows for cross-account aggregation, consolidating findings from integrated AWS services and partner products into a single security account. Designating a delegated administrator account within AWS Organizations enables this central security account to manage Security Hub for the entire organization. Option E (correct) is essential for managing and responding to findings from a central account. By setting up cross-account IAM roles in each member account, the central security account can assume these roles to access and manage resources or findings in those accounts, providing the necessary operational capability without direct login to member accounts. Option B (incorrect) is a valuable tool for enforcing compliance and detecting non-compliance, but its primary purpose is not the aggregation and centralized management of security findings. Option C (incorrect) provides preventive guardrails by defining maximum permissions, which is crucial for overall security, but it does not directly facilitate the aggregation or centralized management of security findings. Option D (incorrect) is used for automating evidence collection for compliance audits, which is a different use case than real-time security finding aggregation and management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_98',
  'A healthcare organization operating under strict regulatory compliance frameworks (e.g., HIPAA) needs to ensure that its AWS environment continuously adheres to a defined set of security and compliance standards. The organization utilizes a multi-account strategy managed by AWS Organizations. The security team requires a solution that can automatically check for non-compliant resource configurations across all accounts and OUs, report on deviations, and ideally provide a mechanism for automated remediation. They want to ensure that specific rules, such as mandatory encryption for all Amazon EBS volumes and restricted public access for Amazon S3 buckets, are consistently enforced and audited. Which two AWS services, when used together, provide the most effective approach to meet these continuous compliance requirements across the organization? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs deployed via AWS Organizations to audit and report on compliance across multiple accounts.","AWS Audit Manager to automate the collection of evidence for compliance assessments.","Amazon Security Hub to aggregate compliance findings from various services and provide a unified compliance score.","AWS Service Catalog to ensure that only pre-approved and compliant resources can be provisioned by users.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy standardized resource configurations across all accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) is highly effective for continuous compliance. AWS Config conformance packs allow the organization to deploy a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single entity across multiple accounts and OUs via AWS Organizations. This automates the auditing of resource configurations against desired compliance standards (like mandatory encryption for EBS or S3 public access) and can trigger remediation. Option C (correct) complements Config conformance packs by providing a centralized view of compliance. Amazon Security Hub aggregates security findings, including compliance checks from AWS Config, and maps them against industry standards and best practices (like CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark), providing a unified compliance score and actionable insights across the entire organization. Option B (incorrect) is used for automating evidence collection for compliance audits and preparing for external assessments, but it doesn''t perform the continuous, real-time auditing and reporting of resource configurations against specific rules in the same way AWS Config does. Option D (incorrect) is a preventive control that ensures only compliant resources are provisioned, which is valuable, but it doesn''t continuously audit *existing* resources for drift or non-compliance against a set of rules. Option E (incorrect) is a deployment tool for infrastructure as code. While it can deploy compliant configurations, it doesn''t inherently provide continuous auditing, reporting, or automated remediation for compliance drift without additional integration with AWS Config.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_99',
  'A large software company manages a complex multi-account AWS environment with hundreds of development teams. The security team needs to establish a robust governance model that prevents developers from inadvertently or intentionally creating resources that violate corporate security policies, such as creating public Amazon S3 buckets or launching unapproved Amazon EC2 instance types. However, developers still require flexibility to innovate within defined boundaries. The solution must ensure that these policies are enforced at the account level, regardless of the IAM permissions granted to individual users or roles within those accounts, and that a baseline of security configurations is consistently applied. Which two AWS Organizations features, when combined, provide the most effective preventive controls for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing Service Control Policies (SCPs) at the Organizational Unit (OU) level to define maximum permissions for member accounts.","Utilizing AWS Config conformance packs to audit and report on compliance against desired security baselines.","Designing a granular Organizational Unit (OU) hierarchy to group accounts with similar security requirements and apply targeted policies.","Delegating administration of Amazon Security Hub to a central security account for cross-account aggregation of findings.","Enabling AWS Control Tower''s Account Factory to provision new accounts with a pre-configured baseline and guardrails."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) is crucial for enforcing preventive controls. Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations allow the security team to define the maximum permissions that any IAM user or role within an account can have. By attaching SCPs to OUs or individual accounts, the company can prevent actions like creating public S3 buckets or launching unapproved EC2 instance types, regardless of the IAM permissions granted inside the account. Option C (correct) is essential for effective policy application. Designing a granular OU hierarchy allows the security team to group accounts based on their function, sensitivity, or compliance needs. This enables the application of targeted SCPs and other policies, providing flexibility for different development teams while maintaining strict control where necessary. Option B (incorrect) is a detective control that audits and reports on compliance *after* resources are configured. While important for continuous monitoring, it does not *prevent* non-compliant actions from occurring in the first place, which is a key requirement for preventive controls. Option D (incorrect) is for centralizing security findings and monitoring, which is a detective capability. It does not provide preventive controls to stop non-compliant actions. Option E (incorrect) is a powerful tool for automating the provisioning of new accounts with a baseline and guardrails, which is a form of preventive control. However, the question specifically asks for *two AWS Organizations features*. While Control Tower leverages Organizations, the Account Factory itself is a Control Tower feature, not solely an Organizations feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_100',
  'A rapidly expanding startup is adopting a multi-account strategy using AWS Organizations to isolate workloads and environments. They currently use a single AWS account for all development, testing, and production, which has led to challenges in managing access control, enforcing consistent security policies, and containing security incidents. The security team wants to transition to a multi-account structure to improve their security posture, reduce blast radius, and simplify compliance scoping. Which two benefits are directly realized by moving from a single AWS account to a well-architected multi-account strategy using AWS Organizations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enhanced blast radius containment, limiting the impact of a security incident to a specific account or workload.","Simplified compliance scoping by applying specific policies to OUs and accounts based on their regulatory requirements.","Reduced operational overhead by consolidating all resources into a single billing entity.","Improved performance for applications by distributing workloads across multiple isolated accounts.","Automatic encryption of all data at rest and in transit across all accounts within the organization."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a primary benefit of a multi-account strategy. By isolating workloads and environments into separate accounts, the impact of a security incident (e.g., a compromised credential or misconfigured resource) is contained within that specific account, preventing it from affecting other critical workloads or environments. Option B (correct) is also a significant advantage. A multi-account structure, especially when combined with Organizational Units (OUs) in AWS Organizations, allows for simplified compliance scoping. Policies and controls can be applied specifically to accounts or OUs that fall under certain regulatory requirements, making it easier to demonstrate and manage compliance. Option C (incorrect) is a common misconception. While AWS Organizations can consolidate billing for multiple accounts, the primary benefit is not reduced operational overhead in general, especially for security and compliance, which often requires more granular management. A single account typically has lower operational overhead for billing, but higher security risk. Option D (incorrect) is not a direct benefit of a multi-account strategy itself. While distributing workloads can improve performance, it''s an architectural decision within the accounts, not an inherent benefit of the multi-account structure for security governance. Option E (incorrect) is not automatically provided by adopting a multi-account strategy. While AWS services offer encryption capabilities, it requires explicit configuration (e.g., S3 bucket encryption, EBS encryption) within each account, and is not an automatic feature of AWS Organizations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_101',
  'A large enterprise is migrating to AWS and needs to establish a secure, scalable, and centrally governed multi-account environment, including automated provisioning of new accounts with pre-configured guardrails. Which AWS services are foundational for achieving this objective? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS CloudFormation StackSets","AWS Control Tower","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Organizations"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (AWS Control Tower) is correct because it automates the setup of a new, secure multi-account AWS environment (a landing zone) based on AWS best practices, including account provisioning and guardrails. Option E (AWS Organizations) is also correct as it is the underlying service that enables central management of multiple AWS accounts, including consolidated billing and the application of Service Control Policies (SCPs). AWS Control Tower builds on AWS Organizations. Option A (Amazon S3) is an object storage service and not directly used for multi-account governance. Option B (AWS CloudFormation StackSets) is used for deploying resources consistently across multiple accounts and Regions, but it doesn''t provide the full landing zone setup and governance capabilities of Control Tower. Option D (AWS Service Catalog) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, enabling compliant resource provisioning, but it is not foundational for setting up the multi-account structure itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_102',
  'A security team needs to enforce mandatory security policies across all accounts within their AWS Organization to prevent non-compliant resource deployments and ensure adherence to a baseline security posture. Which AWS Organizations features are crucial for implementing these preventive controls? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Audit Manager","Organizational Units (OUs)","AWS Config conformance packs","Service Control Policies (SCPs)","Amazon Security Hub"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Organizational Units (OUs)) is correct because OUs allow for hierarchical grouping of AWS accounts, enabling the application of policies to groups of accounts rather than individual ones, simplifying management. Option D (Service Control Policies (SCPs)) is also correct as SCPs are a type of policy that you can use to manage permissions in your organization, acting as guardrails that define the maximum available permissions for accounts within an OU or the entire organization. SCPs are preventive controls that restrict actions before they occur. Option A (AWS Audit Manager) is used for automated evidence collection for compliance audits, which is a detective control. Option C (AWS Config conformance packs) are used to assess and report on compliance of resources, which is a detective control. Option E (Amazon Security Hub) aggregates security findings and provides a consolidated view of security posture, which is a detective control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_103',
  'A security team is responsible for monitoring compliance status and aggregating security findings across a multi-account AWS environment to maintain a strong security posture. Which services can effectively centralize compliance status reporting and aggregate security findings? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Service Catalog","Amazon Security Hub","AWS CloudFormation StackSets","AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Audit Manager"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Amazon Security Hub) is correct because it provides a comprehensive view of your security alerts and security posture across your AWS accounts, aggregating findings from various AWS services and partner products. Option D (AWS Config conformance packs) is also correct as they provide a general-purpose compliance framework that allows you to create a package of AWS Config rules and remediation actions to deploy across an organization, enabling multi-account compliance status reporting. Option A (AWS Service Catalog) is for provisioning approved resources, not for aggregating security findings or compliance status. Option C (AWS CloudFormation StackSets) is for deploying resources consistently, not for monitoring compliance or aggregating findings. Option E (AWS Audit Manager) helps automate evidence collection for audits, which is related to compliance but not primarily for real-time security finding aggregation or continuous compliance status reporting across an organization in the same way Security Hub and Config conformance packs do.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_104',
  'An organization plans to use a single AWS account for all development, testing, and production workloads to simplify initial setup. Which of the following are inherent security risks associated with this anti-pattern (ap-6.1-001)? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Absence of environment isolation","Increased cost of AWS services due to lack of separation","Lack of blast radius containment","Complexity in integrating third-party security tools for a single account","Reduced performance for critical applications due to shared resources"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Absence of environment isolation) is correct because a single account for all environments (dev, test, prod) means that a misconfiguration or breach in one environment could easily impact others, making it difficult to maintain strict separation of duties and access controls. Option C (Lack of blast radius containment) is also correct. A single account provides no natural boundary to contain the impact of a security incident, meaning a compromise could affect all workloads and data within that account. Option B (Increased cost of AWS services due to lack of separation) is generally incorrect; a single account might initially seem simpler, but it doesn''t inherently increase service costs and can sometimes lead to less efficient resource utilization, but not necessarily higher costs. Option D (Complexity in integrating third-party security tools for a single account) is incorrect; integrating tools is often simpler with fewer accounts, though a multi-account strategy is better for scaling. Option E (Reduced performance for critical applications due to shared resources) is incorrect; while resource contention can occur, it''s not a direct security risk of a single account strategy, but rather an operational concern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_105',
  'An enterprise needs to rapidly provision new AWS accounts for different teams while ensuring they adhere to a baseline security posture and centralized management. Which AWS services facilitate automated and secure account vending and governance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets","AWS Audit Manager","AWS Organizations","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Control Tower"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (AWS Organizations) is correct because it provides the foundational service for centrally managing multiple AWS accounts, enabling consolidated billing and the application of policies across accounts. Option E (AWS Control Tower) is also correct as it builds on AWS Organizations to provide a simplified way to set up and govern a new, secure, multi-account AWS environment (a landing zone), including automated account provisioning (account vending machine) with pre-configured guardrails. Option A (AWS CloudFormation StackSets) is for deploying resources consistently, not for account vending. Option B (AWS Audit Manager) is for automated evidence collection for audits. Option D (AWS Service Catalog) is for provisioning approved resources within an account, not for provisioning the accounts themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_1_106',
  'A security operations center (SOC) needs a consolidated view of security findings and continuous compliance status across all AWS accounts in their organization. Which AWS services are essential for achieving this centralized visibility and reporting? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Config (for compliance status via conformance packs)","AWS Audit Manager","Amazon Security Hub"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (AWS Config (for compliance status via conformance packs)) is correct because AWS Config continuously monitors resource configurations and allows the deployment of conformance packs across multiple accounts to assess and report on compliance against predefined standards, providing continuous compliance status. Option E (Amazon Security Hub) is also correct as it aggregates security findings from various AWS services (like GuardDuty, Macie, Inspector) and partner solutions across all accounts, providing a centralized and standardized view of security posture. Option A (AWS CloudFormation) is for infrastructure provisioning. Option B (AWS Service Catalog) is for controlled resource provisioning. Option D (AWS Audit Manager) is for automating evidence collection for audits, which is related to compliance but not primarily for real-time security finding aggregation or continuous compliance status reporting across an organization in the same way Security Hub and Config conformance packs do.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_1',
  'medium'
);

