INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_48',
  'A security team needs to continuously evaluate the compliance of AWS resources against internal security standards and industry regulations across multiple accounts. They also require a centralized dashboard to view the compliance status. Which AWS services should they leverage to meet these requirements effectively? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config for continuous monitoring and evaluation of resource configurations.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating compliance findings and providing a dashboard.","AWS Audit Manager for automated evidence collection for audits.","AWS Organizations for managing the account structure.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning compliant resources."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows you to assess, audit, and evaluate the configurations of your AWS resources. It continuously monitors and records your AWS resource configurations and allows you to automate the evaluation of recorded configurations against desired baselines. Option B (correct) collects security data from across AWS accounts, services, and supported third-party products, and helps you analyze your security posture. It provides a centralized dashboard to view compliance status against various security standards. Option C (incorrect) helps automate the collection of evidence for audits but doesn''t provide continuous monitoring or a real-time compliance dashboard. Option D (incorrect) is for managing multiple AWS accounts, not for evaluating resource compliance. Option E (incorrect) helps users provision pre-approved AWS resources but is not for evaluating the compliance of existing resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_49',
  'An organization needs to ensure that its AWS resources consistently adhere to a specific set of compliance rules across all accounts in its AWS Organization. They want to deploy these rules uniformly and receive consolidated compliance reports. Which two AWS services can be used together to achieve this goal efficiently? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs for deploying standardized compliance rules.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating compliance findings and generating reports.","AWS Organizations for managing the account structure.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying individual Config rules.","AWS Audit Manager for collecting evidence for external audits."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows you to deploy a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single entity across multiple accounts, ensuring standardized compliance. Option B (correct) collects security data from across AWS accounts, services, and supported third-party products, and helps you analyze your security posture. It aggregates compliance findings from Config and other sources, providing a centralized view and reports. Option C (incorrect) is for managing multiple AWS accounts and applying SCPs, but not directly for deploying compliance rules or generating reports. Option D (incorrect) can deploy individual CloudFormation templates, but conformance packs are specifically designed for deploying a *set* of Config rules for compliance. Option E (incorrect) automates evidence collection for audits but is not the primary service for deploying continuous compliance rules or providing real-time compliance reports.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_50',
  'A compliance officer needs to automate the collection of evidence for regulatory audits (e.g., PCI DSS, HIPAA) across several AWS accounts. The solution must streamline the audit preparation process by continuously gathering relevant data. Which two AWS services are designed to facilitate this automated evidence collection? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Audit Manager for automating evidence collection and assessment reports.","AWS Config for recording resource configurations and compliance status.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings.","AWS Organizations for centralizing account management.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning compliant resources."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) helps you continuously audit your AWS usage to simplify how you assess risk and compliance with regulations and industry standards. It automates evidence collection from various AWS services. Option B (correct) continuously monitors and records your AWS resource configurations and allows you to automate the evaluation of recorded configurations against desired baselines. This configuration history and compliance status serve as critical evidence for audits, which Audit Manager can then collect. Option C (incorrect) aggregates security findings and compliance checks, which can inform an audit, but it doesn''t automate the collection of audit-specific evidence in the way Audit Manager does. Option D (incorrect) is for managing multiple AWS accounts, not for evidence collection. Option E (incorrect) is for provisioning pre-approved resources, not for audit evidence collection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_51',
  'A global organization operates in multiple AWS Regions and has a multi-account structure. The security team wants to centralize all security findings and compliance checks from various AWS services into a single dashboard to gain a comprehensive view of their security posture. Which two capabilities of Amazon Security Hub are crucial for achieving this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Cross-account aggregation of security findings.","Cross-Region aggregation of security findings.","Automated remediation of non-compliant resources.","Deployment of preventive guardrails across accounts.","Continuous recording of resource configuration changes."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows Security Hub to consolidate security findings from all member accounts into a single administrator account, providing a unified view across the organization. Option B (correct) enables Security Hub to aggregate findings from multiple AWS Regions into a single designated aggregation Region, further centralizing the security posture view. Option C (incorrect) is not a direct capability of Security Hub; while Security Hub can integrate with other services for remediation, it does not automate remediation itself. Option D (incorrect) is a function of AWS Organizations with SCPs or AWS Control Tower, not Security Hub. Option E (incorrect) is a primary function of AWS Config, not Security Hub.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_52',
  'A company needs to implement a continuous compliance strategy across its multi-account AWS environment. This strategy requires identifying non-compliant resources, assessing them against industry standards, and generating reports for auditors. Which AWS services are best suited to support these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config for continuous monitoring and compliance evaluation.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating findings and standards-based checks.","AWS Control Tower for setting up a secure landing zone.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning compliant resources.","AWS CloudFormation for deploying infrastructure as code."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations, allowing for automated evaluation against compliance rules and identifying non-compliant resources. Option B (correct) aggregates security findings from various AWS services and performs automated checks against industry standards (e.g., CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark), providing a centralized view of compliance status and generating reports. Option C (incorrect) helps set up a secure multi-account environment with guardrails but is not the primary service for continuous compliance evaluation and reporting of existing resources. Option D (incorrect) facilitates the provisioning of pre-approved resources but doesn''t perform continuous compliance monitoring or reporting. Option E (incorrect) is for deploying infrastructure but not for continuous compliance evaluation or reporting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_53',
  'A company needs to ensure that all newly deployed resources adhere to a set of predefined security policies and that any deviations are promptly identified. They also require a centralized view of compliance status across their multi-account environment. Which two services are most effective for deploying standardized compliance rules and monitoring for non-compliance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs for deploying a collection of rules.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating findings and compliance checks.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning pre-approved resources.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying infrastructure.","AWS Audit Manager for collecting audit evidence."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows you to deploy a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single entity across multiple accounts, ensuring standardized compliance checks for newly deployed resources. Option B (correct) aggregates security findings and compliance checks from various AWS services and industry standards, providing a centralized view of compliance status and helping to identify deviations. Option C (incorrect) helps in provisioning pre-approved resources, which contributes to compliance, but it doesn''t directly deploy compliance rules or monitor for non-compliance of existing resources. Option D (incorrect) is for deploying infrastructure as code, which can be part of a compliant deployment, but it''s not the primary service for defining and monitoring compliance rules. Option E (incorrect) automates evidence collection for audits but is not for deploying compliance rules or real-time monitoring of non-compliance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_54',
  'An organization is looking to enhance its overall security posture by continuously monitoring for security best practice violations and identifying non-compliant resources across its entire AWS environment. They need a service that can aggregate findings from various security services and evaluate resource configurations against predefined standards. Which two AWS services are best suited for these tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings and running security checks.","AWS Config for recording resource configurations and evaluating compliance.","AWS Control Tower for setting up a secure landing zone.","AWS Organizations for centralizing account management.","AWS Audit Manager for automating evidence collection."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a comprehensive view of your security posture across your AWS accounts. It aggregates security findings from various AWS services (like GuardDuty, Inspector) and partner solutions, and performs automated security checks against industry standards and best practices. Option B (correct) continuously monitors and records your AWS resource configurations. It allows you to define rules to evaluate the compliance of your resources against desired configurations and identify non-compliant resources. Option C (incorrect) is for setting up and governing a multi-account environment, not primarily for continuous security monitoring and compliance evaluation of existing resources. Option D (incorrect) is for managing multiple AWS accounts and applying policies, not for security monitoring or compliance evaluation. Option E (incorrect) automates evidence collection for audits but is not for continuous security monitoring or compliance evaluation of resource configurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_55',
  'A large enterprise is undergoing a comprehensive security audit and needs to demonstrate continuous compliance with several industry standards, including PCI DSS and SOC 2. The organization uses a multi-account AWS environment managed by AWS Organizations. The security team is tasked with automating the collection of evidence for these audits, ensuring that resource configurations meet specific compliance requirements, and providing a consolidated view of their compliance posture. They also need to identify non-compliant resources and track their remediation status. Which two AWS services are best suited to meet these requirements for automated evidence collection and continuous compliance monitoring? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Audit Manager to automate evidence collection and generate audit-ready reports against compliance frameworks.","AWS Config conformance packs to define and deploy a set of compliance rules and remediation actions across multiple accounts.","Amazon Security Hub to aggregate compliance findings and provide a unified dashboard for security and compliance posture.","AWS Service Catalog to ensure that only pre-approved and compliant resources can be provisioned by users.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy standardized infrastructure configurations across all accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is specifically designed for automating evidence collection. AWS Audit Manager helps continuously audit your AWS usage to simplify how you assess risk and compliance with regulations and industry standards. It automates the collection of evidence, making it easier to generate audit-ready reports for frameworks like PCI DSS and SOC 2. Option B (correct) is crucial for continuous compliance monitoring and enforcement. AWS Config conformance packs allow the organization to define a set of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single unit and deploy them across multiple accounts via AWS Organizations. This ensures that resource configurations are continuously audited against compliance requirements and deviations are identified. Option C (incorrect) aggregates security findings and provides a unified dashboard, including compliance scores. While it consumes findings from AWS Config, its primary role is not automated evidence collection for audit reports, which is Audit Manager''s strength. Option D (incorrect) is a preventive control for provisioning compliant resources. It ensures that new resources adhere to standards but does not automate evidence collection or continuously monitor existing resources for compliance drift. Option E (incorrect) is a tool for deploying infrastructure as code. While it can deploy compliant configurations, it doesn''t inherently provide continuous auditing, evidence collection, or reporting for compliance without integration with other services like AWS Config.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_56',
  'A large enterprise has a critical requirement to ensure that all Amazon S3 buckets containing sensitive customer data are encrypted at rest and that access to these buckets is restricted to authorized IAM roles only. They operate a multi-account AWS environment and need to continuously monitor for any deviations from these security policies. If a non-compliant S3 bucket is detected (e.g., unencrypted or publicly accessible), the security team wants to be immediately notified and, if possible, have an automated process to revert the configuration to a compliant state. Which two AWS services, when configured together, provide the most effective solution for continuous monitoring and automated remediation of such compliance violations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config rules to continuously evaluate S3 bucket configurations against desired compliance policies.","AWS Lambda functions to automatically remediate non-compliant S3 bucket configurations detected by AWS Config.","Amazon GuardDuty to detect unusual or unauthorized access patterns to S3 buckets.","Amazon Macie to discover, classify, and protect sensitive data stored in S3.","Amazon Security Hub to aggregate findings from AWS Config and other services for a centralized view."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is essential for continuous monitoring. AWS Config rules allow the enterprise to define desired configurations for S3 buckets (e.g., encrypted, not publicly accessible) and continuously evaluate them. Any deviation from these rules triggers a non-compliance finding. Option B (correct) provides the automated remediation capability. AWS Config rules can be configured to trigger AWS Lambda functions when a non-compliant resource is detected. These Lambda functions can then execute predefined actions, such as enabling encryption on an S3 bucket or modifying its public access settings, to automatically revert it to a compliant state. Option C (incorrect) is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior. While important for S3 security, it focuses on threats rather than continuous compliance monitoring of resource configurations and automated remediation of non-compliant settings. Option D (incorrect) is used for discovering and protecting sensitive data in S3. While valuable for data security, it doesn''t directly provide the continuous configuration compliance monitoring or automated remediation capabilities described in the scenario. Option E (incorrect) aggregates security findings from various services, including AWS Config, into a centralized dashboard. While it provides a unified view of compliance, it doesn''t perform the continuous evaluation of resource configurations or the automated remediation actions itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_57',
  'A large enterprise with a multi-account AWS environment needs to establish a robust compliance framework. The security team is responsible for ensuring that all AWS resources adhere to internal security policies and external regulatory standards. They require a solution that can identify non-compliant resources, aggregate these findings into a central dashboard, and provide a clear, actionable view of their overall compliance posture across all accounts. Additionally, the solution must support mapping findings to specific compliance standards (e.g., CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, PCI DSS). Which two AWS services are most effective for achieving centralized compliance visibility and mapping findings to industry standards? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Security Hub to aggregate compliance findings from various services and provide a unified compliance dashboard with standard mappings.","AWS Config conformance packs to define and deploy a set of compliance rules across multiple accounts and OUs.","AWS Audit Manager to automate the collection of evidence for compliance assessments and generate audit reports.","AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) to prevent non-compliant actions at the organizational level.","AWS Service Catalog to ensure that only pre-approved and compliant resources can be provisioned by users."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is ideal for centralized compliance visibility and mapping to standards. Amazon Security Hub aggregates security and compliance findings from AWS Config and other integrated services, providing a unified dashboard. It maps these findings to industry standards (like CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, PCI DSS), offering a clear, actionable view of the overall compliance posture across all accounts. Option B (correct) is crucial for defining and deploying compliance rules across the organization. AWS Config conformance packs allow the enterprise to define a collection of AWS Config rules and deploy them as a single unit across multiple accounts via AWS Organizations. This ensures that resource configurations are continuously audited against internal policies and external standards, generating findings that Security Hub can then aggregate. Option C (incorrect) is primarily for automating evidence collection and generating audit reports for specific assessments, rather than providing a real-time, centralized dashboard of compliance posture. Option D (incorrect) provides preventive controls by defining maximum permissions at the organizational level. While crucial for preventing non-compliant actions, it does not provide a dashboard for compliance posture or aggregate findings. Option E (incorrect) is a preventive control for provisioning compliant resources. It ensures that new resources adhere to standards but does not provide a dashboard for compliance posture or aggregate findings from existing resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_3_58',
  'A large enterprise needs to ensure that its AWS environment continuously adheres to a strict set of security and compliance policies, including specific tagging requirements for all resources, mandatory encryption for data storage, and restricted network access. The organization operates a multi-account structure managed by AWS Organizations. The security team requires a solution that can automatically identify non-compliant resources, track their compliance status over time, and generate detailed reports for internal and external auditors. Which two AWS services, when used together, provide the most effective combination for continuous compliance monitoring, historical tracking, and audit reporting across the entire organization? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config to continuously record configuration changes and evaluate resources against desired compliance rules.","AWS Audit Manager to automate the collection of evidence for compliance assessments and generate audit-ready reports.","Amazon Security Hub to aggregate compliance findings and provide a unified dashboard for security and compliance posture.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy standardized security configurations across all accounts.","AWS Service Catalog to ensure that only pre-approved and compliant resources can be provisioned by users."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is fundamental for continuous compliance monitoring and historical tracking. AWS Config continuously records configuration changes of AWS resources and evaluates them against desired compliance rules (e.g., tagging, encryption, network access). It maintains a configuration history, allowing for tracking compliance status over time. Option B (correct) is specifically designed for audit reporting. AWS Audit Manager automates the collection of evidence from AWS services, including AWS Config, to help prepare for audits. It can generate audit-ready reports against various compliance frameworks, making it easier to demonstrate adherence to policies. Option C (incorrect) aggregates security and compliance findings from various services, including AWS Config, into a centralized dashboard. While it provides a unified view of compliance, its primary role is not automated evidence collection for audit reports or detailed historical tracking of individual resource configurations in the same depth as AWS Config. Option D (incorrect) is a deployment tool for infrastructure as code. While it can deploy compliant configurations, it doesn''t inherently provide continuous auditing, historical tracking, or automated evidence collection for compliance without integration with other services. Option E (incorrect) is a preventive control for provisioning compliant resources. It ensures that new resources adhere to standards but does not continuously monitor existing resources for compliance drift, track historical changes, or automate evidence collection for audits.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_3',
  'hard'
);

