INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_71',
  'Which AWS services enable organizations to provision cloud resources in a secure, consistent, and compliant manner using pre-approved templates? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Service Catalog","AWS CloudFormation","Amazon S3 for object storage","AWS Lambda for serverless compute","Amazon EC2 instances for virtual servers"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Service Catalog) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. These IT services can include everything from virtual machine images, servers, software, and databases to complete multi-tier application architectures. Option B (AWS CloudFormation) allows you to model, provision, and manage AWS and third-party resources by treating infrastructure as code, ensuring consistent and repeatable deployments. Amazon S3 (Option C) is for storage. AWS Lambda (Option D) is for serverless compute. Amazon EC2 instances (Option E) are compute resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_72',
  'A development team requires access to a standardized set of AWS resources (e.g., EC2 instances, S3 buckets) that are pre-configured to meet security and compliance standards. Which services can provide this self-service provisioning while enforcing governance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Organizations for account management","Amazon DynamoDB for NoSQL data","AWS Service Catalog","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Control Tower for multi-account governance"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (AWS Service Catalog) enables organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, allowing development teams to self-service provision compliant resources. Option D (AWS CloudFormation) is used to define and provision the underlying infrastructure as code for these standardized resources, ensuring consistency and adherence to security baselines. AWS Organizations (Option A) and AWS Control Tower (Option E) are for multi-account management and governance, not direct resource provisioning. Amazon DynamoDB (Option B) is a database service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_73',
  'To ensure that deployed AWS resources remain compliant over time and detect any unauthorized changes, which services offer drift detection and continuous monitoring? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Service Catalog for approved product provisioning","AWS Audit Manager for compliance evidence","Amazon SQS for messaging","AWS Config","AWS CloudFormation"]'::json,
  '{3,4}',
  'Option D (AWS Config) continuously monitors and records your AWS resource configurations, allowing you to assess, audit, and evaluate configurations against desired baselines. Option E (AWS CloudFormation) includes drift detection capabilities, allowing you to identify when the actual configuration of a stack''s resources differs from its expected configuration in the CloudFormation template. AWS Service Catalog (Option A) is for provisioning. AWS Audit Manager (Option B) is for compliance evidence collection. Amazon SQS (Option C) is a messaging service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_74',
  'An organization needs to enforce a policy that only allows the deployment of encrypted Amazon EBS volumes. Which services can act as preventive controls to block non-compliant deployments? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config for detective controls","Amazon Security Hub for aggregated findings","AWS Audit Manager for compliance evidence","AWS Organizations SCPs","AWS Service Catalog"]'::json,
  '{3,4}',
  'Option D (AWS Organizations SCPs) can be used to prevent the creation of unencrypted EBS volumes by denying the `ec2:RunInstances` or `ec2:CreateVolume` actions if encryption is not specified or enforced. Option E (AWS Service Catalog) can enforce this by only offering pre-approved products (e.g., EC2 launch templates) that mandate EBS encryption, thereby preventing users from provisioning non-compliant resources. AWS Config (Option A), Amazon Security Hub (Option B), and AWS Audit Manager (Option C) are primarily detective controls (ap-6.2-001) that identify non-compliance after it occurs, rather than preventing it.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_75',
  'Which AWS services help automate the collection of compliance evidence from AWS resources, simplifying audit preparation and reporting? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation for infrastructure as code","AWS Service Catalog for approved product provisioning","Amazon S3 for object storage","AWS Audit Manager","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{3,4}',
  'Option D (AWS Audit Manager) helps you continuously audit your AWS usage to simplify how you assess risk and compliance with regulations and industry standards by automating evidence collection. Option E (AWS Config) continuously monitors and records your AWS resource configurations, providing a historical record and compliance status that can serve as audit evidence. AWS CloudFormation (Option A) is for provisioning. AWS Service Catalog (Option B) is for self-service provisioning. Amazon S3 (Option C) is for object storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_76',
  'An organization needs to ensure that all AWS resources deployed across its multi-account environment are consistently configured according to security best practices. They also want to detect any unauthorized changes to these configurations. Which two AWS services are essential for achieving consistent deployments and detecting configuration drift? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying consistent infrastructure.","AWS Config for continuous monitoring and drift detection.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning pre-approved resources.","AWS Organizations for applying preventive Service Control Policies (SCPs).","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows you to deploy a common CloudFormation stack across multiple AWS accounts and Regions with a single operation, ensuring consistent infrastructure deployments. Option B (correct) continuously monitors and records your AWS resource configurations. It can be used to detect configuration drift by comparing the current configuration of resources against desired baselines or the CloudFormation template, and it also has specific drift detection capabilities for CloudFormation stacks. Option C (incorrect) helps in provisioning pre-approved resources, which supports consistent deployments, but it''s not the primary service for detecting drift in existing resources. Option D (incorrect) provides preventive controls but doesn''t directly handle consistent deployments or drift detection. Option E (incorrect) aggregates security findings, which is a detective control, not a deployment or drift detection mechanism.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_77',
  'A security team is implementing a strategy to ensure that only compliant AWS resources are provisioned and that deployments are standardized across their organization. They also need to collect evidence automatically for compliance audits. Which two AWS services are crucial for achieving compliant provisioning and automated evidence collection? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Service Catalog for provisioning pre-approved, compliant resources.","AWS Audit Manager for automating evidence collection for audits.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying consistent infrastructure.","AWS Config for continuous compliance monitoring.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, ensuring that users can only provision resources that conform to predefined security and compliance standards. Option B (correct) helps continuously audit your AWS usage to simplify how you assess risk and compliance with regulations and industry standards. It automates the collection of evidence from various AWS services. Option C (incorrect) is for deploying consistent infrastructure across accounts but doesn''t inherently provide compliant provisioning or automated evidence collection. Option D (incorrect) is for continuous compliance monitoring, which generates data that can be *used* as evidence, but Audit Manager is the service that automates the *collection* of that evidence. Option E (incorrect) aggregates security findings, which is a detective control, not a provisioning or evidence collection service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_78',
  'An organization wants to enforce a strong security posture by implementing preventive controls that block non-compliant actions before they occur. They also need a mechanism to ensure that resources are provisioned from a set of pre-approved, secure configurations. Which two AWS services are essential for implementing these preventive controls and compliant provisioning? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Organizations with Service Control Policies (SCPs) for preventive controls.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning pre-approved, compliant resources.","AWS Config for detecting non-compliant resources.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying consistent infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows administrators to create Service Control Policies (SCPs) that define the maximum available permissions for accounts within an Organizational Unit (OU), effectively blocking non-compliant actions before they occur. Option B (correct) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, ensuring that users can only provision resources that conform to predefined security and compliance standards. Option C (incorrect) is a detective control that identifies non-compliant resources after they are created, rather than preventing the action. Option D (incorrect) aggregates security findings, which is a detective control, not a preventive control or provisioning service. Option E (incorrect) is for deploying consistent infrastructure, which can be part of a secure deployment, but SCPs provide the organizational-level preventive control, and Service Catalog handles compliant provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_79',
  'A security architect is designing a strategy to deploy standardized and secure infrastructure across multiple AWS accounts and Regions. The goal is to ensure consistency, reduce manual effort, and maintain an auditable deployment process. Which two AWS services are best suited to achieve this consistent, multi-account deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying infrastructure across accounts and Regions.","AWS Service Catalog for providing self-service access to approved infrastructure.","AWS Control Tower for setting up a multi-account landing zone.","AWS Organizations for managing the account structure.","AWS Config for monitoring resource configurations."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) extends the functionality of AWS CloudFormation by enabling you to deploy a common CloudFormation stack across multiple AWS accounts and Regions with a single operation, ensuring consistency and reducing manual effort. Option B (correct) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. This enables users to provision standardized and secure infrastructure in a self-service manner, contributing to a consistent and auditable deployment process. Option C (incorrect) sets up the initial multi-account landing zone and guardrails, but CloudFormation StackSets and Service Catalog are more directly involved in the ongoing deployment of specific infrastructure. Option D (incorrect) is for managing multiple AWS accounts and applying policies, not for deploying infrastructure. Option E (incorrect) is for monitoring resource configurations and compliance, not for deploying infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_80',
  'To ensure a secure and compliant environment, an organization needs to implement both preventive and detective security controls. They want to prevent unauthorized actions at the organizational level and also continuously monitor for configuration deviations across their multi-account setup. Which three AWS services are fundamental for implementing these types of controls? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Organizations with Service Control Policies (SCPs) for preventive controls.","AWS Config for continuous monitoring and detective control over resource configurations.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings and compliance checks (detective).","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning compliant resources.","AWS Audit Manager for automated evidence collection."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for the creation of Service Control Policies (SCPs) that define the maximum available permissions for accounts within an Organizational Unit (OU), acting as a powerful preventive control. Option B (correct) continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations, allowing for the definition of rules to evaluate compliance against desired baselines, thus serving as a key detective control for configuration deviations. Option C (correct) aggregates security findings from various AWS services and performs automated security checks against industry standards, providing a centralized view of the security posture and acting as a comprehensive detective control. Option D (incorrect) helps in provisioning pre-approved resources, which supports compliant deployments, but it''s not a direct preventive or detective control mechanism. Option E (incorrect) automates evidence collection for audits but is not primarily a preventive or detective control for real-time security.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_81',
  'A company needs to establish a secure and consistent multi-account AWS environment. They want to automate the setup of a well-architected landing zone, enforce preventive guardrails, and provide a self-service mechanism for users to provision pre-approved resources. Which three AWS services are essential for achieving these objectives? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Control Tower for automated landing zone setup and guardrails.","AWS Organizations for managing the account structure and SCPs.","AWS Service Catalog for self-service provisioning of approved products.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying consistent infrastructure.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a managed service that sets up a secure, multi-account AWS environment (landing zone), automates account provisioning, and implements preventive and detective guardrails. Option B (correct) is the foundational service that AWS Control Tower uses to manage the organizational structure, create Organizational Units (OUs), and apply Service Control Policies (SCPs) for preventive controls. Option C (correct) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, enabling self-service provisioning of pre-approved resources while adhering to corporate standards. Option D (incorrect) is for deploying infrastructure as code across multiple accounts and Regions but doesn''t provide the full landing zone setup or self-service catalog functionality. Option E (incorrect) aggregates security findings, which is a detective control, not a service for setting up the environment or provisioning resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_82',
  'A security administrator is tasked with ensuring that all new AWS accounts created for development teams adhere to a baseline set of security configurations and that any deviations from these baselines are immediately flagged. They also need to provide a mechanism for developers to deploy common infrastructure components that are pre-approved and secure. Which three AWS services are critical for this secure and consistent deployment strategy? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Control Tower for automated account provisioning and baseline guardrails.","AWS Config conformance packs for deploying and monitoring security baselines.","AWS Service Catalog for provisioning pre-approved, secure infrastructure components.","AWS Organizations for applying preventive Service Control Policies (SCPs).","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (correct) automates the setup of a secure multi-account AWS environment and provides Account Factory for provisioning new accounts with predefined security baselines and guardrails. Option B (correct) allows for the deployment of a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single entity across multiple accounts, ensuring continuous monitoring and flagging of deviations from security baselines. Option C (correct) enables developers to self-service provision pre-approved AWS resources, ensuring that common infrastructure components are deployed securely and consistently. Option D (incorrect) provides preventive controls at the organizational level but is not directly involved in automated account provisioning, baseline monitoring, or self-service provisioning of specific components. Option E (incorrect) aggregates security findings, which is a detective control, not a service for secure deployment or provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_83',
  'A large enterprise wants to standardize its infrastructure deployments across various business units, ensuring that all resources are provisioned with consistent security settings and adhere to corporate policies. They also need to detect any unauthorized changes to these deployed resources. Which three AWS services are fundamental for achieving standardized deployments, policy adherence, and drift detection? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets for deploying consistent infrastructure across accounts.","AWS Service Catalog for providing self-service access to approved infrastructure products.","AWS Config for continuous monitoring of resource configurations and drift detection.","AWS Organizations for enforcing preventive policies via SCPs.","Amazon Security Hub for aggregating security findings."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for deploying a common CloudFormation stack across multiple AWS accounts and Regions with a single operation, ensuring consistent infrastructure deployments. Option B (correct) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. This enables users to provision standardized and secure infrastructure in a self-service manner, ensuring policy adherence. Option C (correct) continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations. It can be used to detect configuration drift by comparing the current configuration of resources against desired baselines or the CloudFormation template, thus identifying unauthorized changes. Option D (incorrect) provides preventive controls at the organizational level, which is crucial for policy adherence, but it''s not directly involved in the deployment or drift detection mechanisms. Option E (incorrect) aggregates security findings, which is a detective control, not a deployment or drift detection mechanism.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_84',
  'An organization is migrating sensitive workloads to AWS and needs to ensure that all resources are deployed securely and that compliance evidence is automatically collected for audit purposes. They also require a centralized way to manage and deploy infrastructure templates across multiple accounts. Which three AWS services are essential for this secure deployment, evidence collection, and centralized template management? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets for centralized deployment of infrastructure templates.","AWS Audit Manager for automated collection of compliance evidence.","AWS Service Catalog for providing pre-approved and secure resource templates.","AWS Config for continuous compliance monitoring.","AWS Organizations for managing account structures."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for deploying a common CloudFormation stack across multiple AWS accounts and Regions with a single operation, providing centralized management and deployment of infrastructure templates. Option B (correct) helps continuously audit your AWS usage to simplify how you assess risk and compliance. It automates the collection of evidence from various AWS services for audit purposes. Option C (correct) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. This ensures that users can provision pre-approved and secure resource templates. Option D (incorrect) is for continuous compliance monitoring, which generates data that can be *used* as evidence, but Audit Manager is the service that automates the *collection* of that evidence. Option E (incorrect) is for managing multiple AWS accounts, not for deployment, evidence collection, or template management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_85',
  'A large enterprise relies heavily on AWS CloudFormation for deploying its infrastructure. The security team has identified a critical need to detect and remediate any unauthorized changes or configuration drifts from the approved CloudFormation templates, especially for production environments. They want to ensure that if a resource''s configuration deviates from its defined template, it is immediately flagged, and ideally, automatically reverted to its compliant state. This is crucial for maintaining a strong security posture and preventing configuration drift that could introduce vulnerabilities. Which two AWS capabilities are most effective for detecting and managing configuration drift in CloudFormation-managed resources? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation drift detection to identify when actual resource configurations differ from their stack templates.","AWS Config rules to continuously evaluate resource configurations against desired state and trigger remediation actions.","AWS CloudTrail to log API calls and track changes made to AWS resources.","Amazon EventBridge to route events from CloudFormation drift detection to a remediation workflow.","AWS Service Catalog to enforce compliant resource provisioning through pre-approved products."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is specifically designed for this purpose. AWS CloudFormation drift detection allows the security team to identify when the actual configuration of stack resources deviates from the configuration defined in their CloudFormation template. This directly addresses the need to flag unauthorized changes. Option B (correct) is highly effective for continuous evaluation and remediation. AWS Config rules can be set up to continuously evaluate resource configurations against desired states (which can be derived from CloudFormation templates). When a non-compliant configuration (drift) is detected, AWS Config can trigger automated remediation actions, such as an AWS Lambda function to revert the resource to its compliant state. Option C (incorrect) logs API calls, which is essential for auditing and forensics, but it does not inherently detect configuration drift from a CloudFormation template or automatically remediate it. It provides the raw data for analysis. Option D (incorrect) is an event bus that can indeed route events, including those from CloudFormation drift detection. However, it''s an orchestration service; it doesn''t perform the drift detection or remediation logic itself. It would be part of a larger solution, but not one of the *most effective capabilities for detecting and managing drift* on its own. Option E (incorrect) is a preventive control that ensures compliant provisioning. It prevents non-compliant resources from being created but does not detect or manage drift in *existing* CloudFormation-managed resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_86',
  'A large enterprise is migrating its legacy applications to AWS and is adopting a ''security by design'' principle. The security team wants to implement strong preventive controls to ensure that developers cannot accidentally or intentionally create resources that violate critical security policies, such as creating public Amazon S3 buckets or launching Amazon EC2 instances without specific encryption settings. They recognize that relying solely on detective controls (like AWS Config) is insufficient for high-risk environments. Which two types of preventive controls are most effective for blocking non-compliant actions before they occur in a multi-account AWS Organizations environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) to define maximum permissions and restrict specific API actions across accounts.","IAM Permission Boundaries to set the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM user or role.","AWS Config rules to continuously evaluate resource configurations against desired compliance standards.","Amazon GuardDuty to continuously monitor for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior.","AWS Service Catalog to offer pre-approved and compliant infrastructure products for self-service provisioning."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a powerful preventive control. AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) define the maximum available permissions for all accounts in an OU or the entire organization. They can explicitly deny actions like creating public S3 buckets or launching EC2 instances without encryption, effectively blocking non-compliant actions before they occur, regardless of IAM permissions within the account. Option B (correct) is another strong preventive control. IAM Permission Boundaries set the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM user or role. This ensures that even if an administrator grants overly permissive policies, the effective permissions are constrained by the boundary, preventing actions that exceed the defined security posture. Option C (incorrect) is a detective control. AWS Config rules continuously evaluate resource configurations *after* they are created and report on non-compliance. While crucial for monitoring, it does not *prevent* the initial non-compliant action. Option D (incorrect) is a detective control. Amazon GuardDuty monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, alerting to potential threats *after* they occur. It does not prevent non-compliant resource creation. Option E (incorrect) is a preventive control for provisioning, ensuring that *new* resources are compliant. However, the question asks for types of preventive controls for *blocking non-compliant actions* more broadly, and SCPs and Permission Boundaries are more fundamental and pervasive for this purpose across an organization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_87',
  'A large enterprise is implementing a secure and consistent deployment strategy for its cloud resources. They want to provide development teams with a self-service portal to provision pre-approved AWS resources, such as Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon RDS databases, and Amazon S3 buckets. The security team needs to ensure that these resources are always provisioned with specific security configurations (e.g., mandatory encryption, private network access, specific tagging). Additionally, they need to enforce that users cannot modify these security-critical parameters during provisioning. Which two types of constraints in AWS Service Catalog are most effective for achieving this controlled and secure self-service provisioning? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Launch constraints to specify an IAM role that AWS Service Catalog assumes when launching a product, enforcing permissions.","Template constraints to prevent users from overriding specific parameters defined in the CloudFormation template.","TagOption Library to enforce mandatory and standardized tags on all provisioned resources.","Notification constraints to send alerts for any attempts to provision non-compliant resources.","StackSet constraints to deploy products across multiple accounts and Regions consistently."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is highly effective for enforcing permissions during provisioning. Launch constraints allow the administrator to specify an IAM role that AWS Service Catalog assumes when launching a product. This ensures that the product is launched with the exact permissions defined by the security team, preventing users from launching resources with excessive or unauthorized privileges. Option B (correct) is crucial for preventing users from modifying security-critical parameters. Template constraints allow administrators to define specific values for CloudFormation template parameters or prevent users from modifying certain parameters altogether. This ensures that mandatory encryption, private network access, or other security configurations cannot be overridden by the user during provisioning. Option C (incorrect) refers to the TagOption Library, which helps enforce mandatory and standardized tags. While important for governance and security, it''s a feature for tag management rather than a ''constraint type'' that directly controls resource parameters or launch permissions in the same way as launch and template constraints. Option D (incorrect) is not a standard constraint type in AWS Service Catalog. While you can integrate Service Catalog with other services for notifications, it''s not a native constraint for preventing non-compliant provisioning. Option E (incorrect) is not a constraint type within Service Catalog. StackSets are used for deploying CloudFormation templates across multiple accounts/regions, often in conjunction with Service Catalog, but they are not a constraint type *within* Service Catalog itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_88',
  'A large enterprise needs to deploy a standardized security configuration, including Amazon S3 bucket policies, AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key policies, and AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles, across 50 development accounts and 10 production accounts, spanning three different AWS Regions. The security team requires a highly automated and consistent deployment mechanism that can apply these configurations uniformly, minimize manual errors, and scale efficiently. The solution must also support updating these configurations centrally. Which two AWS services are most effective for achieving this large-scale, consistent, and automated deployment of security configurations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy CloudFormation templates across multiple accounts and Regions from a central administrator account.","AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) to define maximum permissions and restrict specific API actions across accounts.","AWS Service Catalog to provide a self-service portal for provisioning pre-approved and compliant resources.","AWS Config conformance packs to continuously audit resource configurations against compliance rules.","AWS Lambda functions triggered by Amazon EventBridge to automate configuration changes in target accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is highly effective for large-scale, consistent deployment. AWS CloudFormation StackSets enable the deployment of CloudFormation templates to specified accounts and Regions simultaneously from a central administrator account. This ensures that standardized security configurations (S3 policies, KMS key policies, IAM roles) are applied uniformly and efficiently across all target accounts and Regions. Option B (correct) is crucial for enforcing a security baseline at the organizational level. While StackSets deploy configurations, SCPs in AWS Organizations define the maximum permissions and can restrict specific API actions, acting as a preventive guardrail. For example, an SCP could prevent any account from creating an S3 bucket without encryption, complementing the StackSet deployment by ensuring a foundational security posture. Option C (incorrect) is for self-service provisioning of pre-approved resources. While it can use CloudFormation templates, its primary purpose is not the large-scale, automated deployment of security configurations across many existing accounts and Regions. Option D (incorrect) is a detective control for continuous auditing. It identifies non-compliant resources but does not perform the initial deployment of security configurations. Option E (incorrect) can automate configuration changes but is typically used for targeted, event-driven remediation or smaller-scale automation, not for the initial large-scale, consistent deployment of complex infrastructure as code across many accounts and regions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_89',
  'A security architect needs to ensure consistent and secure deployment of infrastructure across multiple AWS accounts and Regions, and also detect any unauthorized changes to the deployed resources. Which AWS CloudFormation capabilities are best suited for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Drift detection","AWS Service Catalog integration","CloudFormation StackSets","AWS Config rules for post-deployment validation","Amazon S3 for template storage"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Drift detection) is correct because it allows you to detect when the actual configuration of a stack''s resources differs from its expected configuration in the CloudFormation template, helping identify unauthorized changes. Option C (CloudFormation StackSets) is also correct as it enables you to deploy a common set of CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and Regions from a single CloudFormation template, ensuring consistency. Option B (AWS Service Catalog integration) allows for compliant provisioning but is not a CloudFormation capability itself. Option D (AWS Config rules for post-deployment validation) is a detective control that can identify non-compliant resources after deployment, but it''s not a direct CloudFormation capability for consistent deployment or drift detection. Option E (Amazon S3 for template storage) is a storage location for templates but doesn''t provide deployment consistency or drift detection features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_90',
  'To enable developers to provision pre-approved AWS resources while adhering to organizational compliance standards and security guardrails, which AWS services should be utilized for controlled provisioning and policy enforcement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Audit Manager","AWS Organizations (SCPs)","AWS CloudFormation (standalone templates)","AWS Service Catalog","Amazon S3 (for template storage)"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (AWS Organizations (SCPs)) is correct because Service Control Policies (SCPs) act as preventive guardrails, defining the maximum permissions for accounts within an organization, thus enforcing compliance at a foundational level. Option D (AWS Service Catalog) is also correct as it allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, enabling developers to provision compliant resources without direct access to underlying infrastructure definitions. Option A (AWS Audit Manager) is for automated evidence collection for audits, not for provisioning. Option C (AWS CloudFormation (standalone templates)) can define resources but lacks the governance and approval workflows of Service Catalog for controlled provisioning. Option E (Amazon S3 (for template storage)) is merely a storage service and does not provide provisioning or policy enforcement capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_91',
  'To establish a robust security posture, an organization must implement controls that prevent non-compliant actions before they occur, rather than just detecting them after the fact. Which of the following are examples of preventive security controls in AWS? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon GuardDuty","IAM Permission Boundaries","AWS Config rules","Service Control Policies (SCPs)","Amazon CloudWatch Logs"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (IAM Permission Boundaries) is correct because they set the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM entity, effectively preventing entities from performing actions outside these boundaries. Option D (Service Control Policies (SCPs)) is also correct as SCPs are policies that you can use to manage permissions in your organization, acting as guardrails that define the maximum available permissions for accounts, thereby preventing disallowed actions. Option A (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service, making it a detective control. Option C (AWS Config rules) are used to assess and audit the configurations of your AWS resources, making them detective controls (though they can trigger corrective actions). Option E (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a monitoring service for logs, which is primarily a detective control for auditing and analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_92',
  'An organization relies heavily on Amazon GuardDuty and AWS Config rules to identify security misconfigurations and threats. While valuable, what is the primary classification of these types of security controls, and what critical aspect do they primarily address? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Preventive controls","Manual controls","Detective controls","Corrective controls","Automated controls"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (Detective controls) is correct because both Amazon GuardDuty and AWS Config rules primarily work by monitoring and identifying security issues or non-compliance after they have occurred or after a resource has been configured. Option E (Automated controls) is also correct as both services operate automatically without manual intervention to continuously scan and analyze your AWS environment. Option A (Preventive controls) is incorrect; preventive controls, such as SCPs or IAM permission boundaries, block non-compliant actions before they occur. Option B (Manual controls) is incorrect as both services are automated. Option D (Corrective controls) is incorrect as while AWS Config can be configured to trigger automated remediation actions (making it corrective in some cases), its primary classification is detective, and GuardDuty is purely detective.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_59_m_59_2_93',
  'A company wants to enforce that all new AWS resources deployed via CloudFormation StackSets across its organization comply with specific security policies and guardrails. Which mechanisms can be used to ensure these deployments adhere to organizational security standards? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Security Hub","AWS Audit Manager","CloudFormation StackSets (with secure templates)","AWS Config conformance packs","Service Control Policies (SCPs)"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (CloudFormation StackSets (with secure templates)) is correct because deploying resources using pre-approved, secure CloudFormation templates via StackSets ensures consistency and adherence to defined security standards at the point of deployment. Option E (Service Control Policies (SCPs)) is also correct as SCPs are preventive controls within AWS Organizations that define the maximum permissions for accounts, effectively preventing any CloudFormation StackSet deployment from creating resources that violate these overarching organizational policies. Option A (Amazon Security Hub) aggregates security findings and is a detective control. Option B (AWS Audit Manager) automates evidence collection for audits. Option D (AWS Config conformance packs) assess compliance after deployment and are detective controls.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain59_12',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_59_2',
  'medium'
);

